WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.840
Mike Tyson's Punch-Out.

00:00:02.020 --> 00:00:04.840
Meet the Ultimate Challenge Fight, Mike Tyson.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:27.840
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:29.020
I'm Mike.

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:30.320
I'm Sam. I'm Joe.

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:36.440
And I'm Sean. And today we're talking about, if you haven't guessed it from the double intro, Mike Tyson's Punch-Out.

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:38.460
Guys, what do we know about Mike Tyson?

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:41.280
I feel like we've learned a lot recently by playing the game.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:50.420
You know, I know that he was a very controversial boxing man who threatened to eat people's children.

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:52.300
And eventually like ate someone's ear.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:53.600
Eats ears. Yeah, bit an ear.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:55.360
Stars in the Hangover.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:58.340
I feel like that's how I know him the best.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:02.840
Yeah. Because I had never watched boxing or anything, but I did watch The Hangover a lot when it came out.

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:04.540
Yeah. And he was a big deal.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:11.100
And what's interesting is Nintendo signed Mike Tyson to this deal before he even became like the world boxing champion.

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:14.120
So that was a good get for them, I guess.

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:21.880
They just said, I think it was someone from Nintendo of America went to a boxing fight, saw Mike Tyson and was like, this guy's a big deal.

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:22.840
Yeah, he's a big deal.

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:24.380
Like, let's get him a contract.

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:25.840
And then they signed him.

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:28.320
And six months later, he became the world boxing champion.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.200
And so good get for Nintendo.

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:34.120
Yeah, you're right to say that he's a controversial figure now.

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:37.640
But at the time, he was the biggest deal in sports.

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:40.100
I mean, you had Jordan in basketball.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:41.540
You had Gretzky on the ice.

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:43.580
Who's like the baseball?

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:45.540
What game was on ice? Ken Griffey, I guess.

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:46.560
Ken Griffey is all I was going to say.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:48.560
Wait, were you saying Ken Griffey on ice?

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:50.060
No, no, it's the sport.

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:51.680
It's the sport on ice.

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:53.300
That's hockey. Oh, yes.

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:54.800
We haven't gotten a hockey game yet on the NES.

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:55.700
I know, I'm looking forward to one.

00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:57.520
So, is that what you were alluding to?

00:01:57.900 --> 00:01:59.460
No, I just wanted to make a joke.

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:01.000
Okay. Got it.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:04.220
Was there any football equivalent to those other sports?

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:06.580
I guess like Bo, but I don't think...

00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:07.720
But he was also the baseball guy, right?

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:08.920
I guess he was also baseball.

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:10.520
Yeah, that's interesting. Jim Kelly.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:12.040
Jim Kelly.

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:14.260
Specifically and only Jim Kelly.

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:15.300
Shout out Buffalo.

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:16.300
And Thurman Thomas.

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:17.940
Anyway. Anyway, yeah.

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:19.280
To get back to Mike Tyson.

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:21.660
So, this is Mike Tyson's punch out.

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:24.820
There was punch out on the arcade, which was just punch out.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:26.580
Then it becomes Mike Tyson's punch out.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:28.860
Then it goes back to being just punch out.

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:31.600
But that's not because of anything that Mike Tyson did.

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:35.700
He was in jail around that, like a couple years later.

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:37.720
But the contract had just ended.

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:39.800
So, they had to go back to calling it just punch out.

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:42.920
They weren't going to, now that he's the world boxing champion and such a big deal,

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:46.080
they're not going to give him a huge sum of money to keep the license around.

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:48.780
So, they just said, well, what if we just change Mike Tyson to Mr.

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:51.020
Dream? Yeah, redacted's punch out.

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:55.880
Yep. So, had any of you guys played Mike Tyson's punch out before this record?

00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:58.880
It's weird because this, well, before this record, yes.

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:00.120
That was a true question.

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:02.100
I got you.

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:04.980
No, it's weird because this is like one of those games where it's like,

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:10.740
of course I know about it, but I just always assumed it's one of those games

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:13.600
that everyone has this crazy nostalgia for but doesn't really hold up.

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:22.020
So, this was the first time I've played it and I was pleasantly surprised that it was not quite what I was thinking it was.

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:26.380
Well, you know, that's the thing with the NES games is a lot of them, as Sam would say, they're janky.

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:32.200
You know, it's like they used to be good games, but now over time they just haven't quite matured.

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:34.140
But this one feels pretty good.

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:35.580
Would you guys agree? For sure.

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:37.880
This was my first time playing any punch out game.

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:44.160
So, it was kind of, it was weird because it was new, but it was also exactly what I expected.

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:57.500
Huh. Okay. I would say that, yeah, punch out was always just a game that sort of in the same mold as, what was the game that was in that we have already played?

00:03:58.020 --> 00:03:59.140
Ranking? No.

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:01.940
The game that was featured in Back to the Future.

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:04.120
Oh, Hogan's Alley.

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:05.800
Okay. No. The other one.

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:06.860
Urban Champion?

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:08.500
The Western one.

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:09.720
Oh, wow.

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:11.080
Yeah. I'm thinking of Hogan's Alley.

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:12.560
Yeah. You're thinking of.

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:14.260
Oh, shoot. We did a whole podcast.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:16.400
Wild Gunman?

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:17.300
Wild Gunman.

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:31.680
In the same mold as Wild Gunman, it's just the iconography and the imagery of the game are plastered into our cultural mind because we've seen that movie and we've seen it played in other places, but it was a game I never played.

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:33.340
That was sort of what punch out was to me.

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:35.900
I just knew the wireframe for Super Punch Out.

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:42.300
I had played Super Punch Out very briefly before this one, only because I have a Super NES classic.

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:45.780
And we all know Little Mac from Super Smash Bros.

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:47.480
Yeah. Which is an interesting take.

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:51.560
Yeah. So, yeah, this is my first time playing the original.

00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:54.940
Yeah. And so I mentioned Punch Out started as an arcade game.

00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:57.340
Genyo Takata produced that.

00:04:57.540 --> 00:05:04.520
Miyamoto actually designed the characters, including Little Mac, although in the arcade version, Little Mac is a wireframe character.

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:08.040
He's not a small little guy, so I guess you can't even call him Little Mac.

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:09.920
They don't call him that in the arcade game.

00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:10.780
Mecha Mac. Yeah.

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:20.500
Wire Mac. So there's that, but also there's like a split screen version or the only version of the arcade game.

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:22.060
It plays on two separate TVs.

00:05:22.420 --> 00:05:30.380
So there's your TV that you look at where all the boxing match is happening, and then there's another TV above that where all the stats and the meters are.

00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:35.540
And it's kind of interesting that they could, you know, it's almost like a DS look to it, where there's a top and bottom screen.

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:42.260
So if you wanted to play that, you know, on MAME or something, you would need to turn your monitor like vertically.

00:05:42.620 --> 00:05:46.600
Interesting. Which, yeah, is not many games have that going for them outside of shmups.

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:48.240
A lot of shmups do vertical, but.

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:57.140
Oh, yeah. So Takata was known for Popeye and StarTropics, but this game is something that he produced before it ever got big on the NES.

00:05:57.580 --> 00:06:00.040
Is StarTropics a game that comes out in North America?

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:02.580
Yes. And it only comes out in North America.

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:03.960
I think that's right.

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:06.480
I remember. I think it came out in Europe, too, to be honest.

00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:14.940
But the arcade version has Little Mac as a wireframe character, and he's unnamed, and Koji Kondo did all the music for the game.

00:06:15.140 --> 00:06:16.160
So that's pretty cool.

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:17.960
But it's also his first game as a composer.

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:23.840
So we've already been introduced to him through Legend of Zelda and some Mario Brothers, but Punch-Out was where he got his start.

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:25.820
Sam, any music thoughts on this game?

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:27.060
Music is good in this game.

00:06:27.460 --> 00:06:28.800
I'm a big fan. Joe?

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:37.000
It gives you, like, it's really just the one track while you're fighting, but it gives you, like, an on-the-edge-of-your-seat sort of feel.

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:39.400
It really gives you attention.

00:06:39.500 --> 00:06:41.400
It's some pump-up music, for sure.

00:06:41.780 --> 00:06:48.340
Yeah. And this was a Nintendo R&D3 joint, which we don't really ever talk about them too much.

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:50.360
It's usually R&D1 or R&D4.

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:53.640
So for R&D3 to come in here and hit something is interesting.

00:06:54.020 --> 00:06:58.620
Their primary responsibilities were for technical hardware and design.

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:06.920
So I guess that's how the Punch-Out, it just seems like, oh, if you're going to develop this thing for two TVs, might as well have the R&D3 division do the hardware and the software part of it.

00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:08.700
So there's that.

00:07:08.840 --> 00:07:14.500
But more importantly, R&D3 was also credited with the creation of the backup memory battery to save NES games.

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:19.100
Obviously, the first implementation of that being The Legend of Zelda, which we just played a few podcasts ago.

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:22.700
Did this game have that for saving records?

00:07:22.920 --> 00:07:23.800
It did not.

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:32.020
And I think that's interesting because it came out in the United States after Zelda, but they just chose not to include a battery on it.

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:34.460
But the password system is pretty good.

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:36.960
I think it was because there's not too much for it to remember.

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:39.000
It's either where were you in the...

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:40.160
And what's your record.

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:42.100
Yeah, exactly. So it's not like there's...

00:07:42.100 --> 00:07:47.140
Whereas Metroid had a very long password that you had to fill in each time, and that was a little TV.

00:07:47.140 --> 00:07:50.060
You don't have an inventory or anything that needs to remember or anything like that.

00:07:50.120 --> 00:07:54.240
We haven't played it, but I know Castlevania II has one of the longest ever.

00:07:54.460 --> 00:07:58.700
Oh, no. Yeah, it's something like 30 characters.

00:08:00.200 --> 00:08:02.520
So, okay. So that's that on the arcade game.

00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:03.940
We are not here to talk about the arcade game.

00:08:04.040 --> 00:08:05.320
We're here to talk about the NES game.

00:08:05.480 --> 00:08:09.640
I figured we'd do a little Tales from the Manual section here just to kind of give the story.

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:11.220
You play as Little Mac.

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:15.060
He was born in the Bronx, which is in New York City for those that don't know.

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:17.920
17 years old, 107 pounds.

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:19.100
Who let this kid in the ring?

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:26.080
You commented on this earlier, Sean, about how he's fighting Piston Honda in there,

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:29.440
and that guy is just this mammoth of a man.

00:08:30.020 --> 00:08:33.700
And then you have this 107-pound little boy in the ring, and the rest is like...

00:08:33.700 --> 00:08:34.820
He's not even 18 yet.

00:08:35.960 --> 00:08:38.440
The thing is, Mike, that I don't think I told you that.

00:08:38.580 --> 00:08:39.860
That might have been Joe. Oh, okay.

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:45.040
But it's just that whole idea of kids like you.

00:08:45.620 --> 00:08:46.840
Like, these kids just like you.

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:48.600
Yeah. Right, yeah. It's an underdog story.

00:08:48.700 --> 00:08:54.680
Yeah. Because really, most of the enemies, not all of them, but most of them are at least more than twice your size.

00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:56.540
Like, you're as big as their feet.

00:08:57.060 --> 00:09:00.020
And I'm not sure if you guys know this, but I'll go ahead and spoil it.

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:04.660
That was obviously done because they couldn't recreate the wireframe in the NES version,

00:09:04.660 --> 00:09:10.460
so they made him small so that this way there was a way to see what your opponent was doing without...

00:09:10.460 --> 00:09:13.040
Like, that's the whole reason he was wireframing in the arcade version.

00:09:13.220 --> 00:09:16.840
So you could see through him. So in the arcade version, he was like an appropriately sized person.

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:17.980
Appropriately sized person, yes.

00:09:17.980 --> 00:09:20.240
And so in this one, they make him very small.

00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:21.200
So this way it's...

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:23.500
I like the theory that he's you.

00:09:23.740 --> 00:09:25.100
If you're small, you're a kid.

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:27.560
You could beat Hippo King.

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:30.220
Oh, God. But, yeah.

00:09:30.380 --> 00:09:32.500
So in this one, he's just small.

00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:38.920
And I like that they use that as a design choice because his reaches when he goes for uppercuts,

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:40.800
it's really cool how it translates.

00:09:41.180 --> 00:09:44.620
It doesn't feel like Michael Jordan extending his arm in Space Jam.

00:09:44.840 --> 00:09:47.540
You know? It actually looks like the arm connects.

00:09:47.980 --> 00:09:49.940
Yeah. So that's that.

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:53.220
And there's not much of a story to the game other than you're just kind of working through the circuits.

00:09:53.540 --> 00:09:58.340
But there is that Doc Lewis character who, you know...

00:09:58.340 --> 00:09:59.360
Who gives you advice. Yeah.

00:09:59.360 --> 00:10:00.500
You're a trainer. You're a corner.

00:10:00.920 --> 00:10:07.360
Yeah. And, you know, it's weird to think that he's like some 40-year-old man who found this 17-year-old kid and said...

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:11.380
He's a phenom. Of course he's going to find some attention if he can fight with the big guys.

00:10:11.560 --> 00:10:14.580
I can't really stop thinking about the Rocky parallels, right?

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:17.120
There's a lot of Rocky going on in this punch-out thing.

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:18.400
There is. And Rocky was 1979.

00:10:18.940 --> 00:10:26.600
Honestly, I thought that it was going to actually take place in Philadelphia because in between matches or in between circuits, I don't remember.

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:34.000
Yeah, circuits. There will be these little cut scenes of him basically doing the Rocky run, like from the montages.

00:10:34.260 --> 00:10:35.760
But then you see the Statue of Liberty.

00:10:35.800 --> 00:10:38.220
So I guess that ties in with the Bronx.

00:10:38.480 --> 00:10:41.820
But I just thought, you know, I thought they could throw it back to Rocky.

00:10:43.020 --> 00:10:47.700
And since we're in the New York City thing and we're talking about that, Sam, you had a little thought about those cut scenes.

00:10:47.820 --> 00:10:52.480
Well, we were talking about where that takes place because when you see...

00:10:52.480 --> 00:11:01.400
Originally, I was thinking that he was somewhere on the Upper West Side looking at Newark across the river.

00:11:01.780 --> 00:11:03.980
And then I realized, no, he's not there.

00:11:04.520 --> 00:11:09.160
But the Statue of Liberty comes from the left side, which means he's going south.

00:11:09.420 --> 00:11:12.800
He is going south. So Mike and I were talking, we think he's Staten Island.

00:11:13.060 --> 00:11:18.280
Hey, I think he's in Staten Island, but I still don't see why he wouldn't see the Twin Towers.

00:11:18.360 --> 00:11:21.780
So the thing is that we're now just going to debate about the geography.

00:11:22.700 --> 00:11:26.180
Yeah, based on... Well, I'll tell you one thing, even though he was born in the Bronx, he's definitely not in the Bronx.

00:11:26.300 --> 00:11:27.400
No, he's not. Of course not.

00:11:27.540 --> 00:11:32.560
For the moments where I thought he was running on the Upper West Side, I thought it was really cool because that's where I run.

00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:35.440
And I see a similar view when I'm running.

00:11:35.580 --> 00:11:36.920
I don't see the Statue of Liberty.

00:11:37.180 --> 00:11:38.100
A picturesque Newark.

00:11:38.140 --> 00:11:39.620
He's just a 17-year-old boy like me.

00:11:40.060 --> 00:11:42.100
Exactly. Yeah. No, I thought that was cool.

00:11:42.140 --> 00:11:44.720
And so we started talking about it and I realized, no, it's probably not.

00:11:44.720 --> 00:11:47.300
Now it's a dog shit game because of poor...

00:11:47.300 --> 00:11:49.540
I did think it was weird that they didn't include a map.

00:11:49.720 --> 00:11:50.540
There should be a map.

00:11:51.900 --> 00:11:55.700
Yeah, I thought it was weird that you don't get a choice either of your signing bonuses.

00:11:55.700 --> 00:11:57.300
And you're into the next world circuits.

00:11:57.480 --> 00:11:59.940
There's no money that you can use from your championships.

00:12:00.280 --> 00:12:01.100
Yeah, it's very unrealistic.

00:12:01.280 --> 00:12:02.880
I would like to...

00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:10.480
I don't know if this is a tangent, but I would like to state that I like the framing of this entire game.

00:12:10.700 --> 00:12:17.500
I like it when games create a story out of just the structure of sports.

00:12:17.500 --> 00:12:23.700
Whether it's franchise mode or season mode or going through a circuit.

00:12:24.780 --> 00:12:33.040
It's sort of the small stories that this game presents, and they're very small stories, fit really well into that sort of mold.

00:12:33.200 --> 00:12:39.280
But even with the other characters, because you'll beat Piston Honda and he was the original champion of the first circuit.

00:12:39.380 --> 00:12:43.020
And then he shows up two circuits later and he's a lot better too.

00:12:43.020 --> 00:12:46.280
So there's even a story there about how even your villains...

00:12:46.280 --> 00:12:47.100
Villains.

00:12:47.980 --> 00:12:52.700
It's like referring to Mark McGuire as a villain of baseball, right?

00:12:54.200 --> 00:12:55.480
So no, they're not villains.

00:12:55.600 --> 00:13:02.260
But the other fighters, they've worked up too to get better and develop new strategies and stuff like that.

00:13:02.360 --> 00:13:07.200
They could have easily just taken Piston Honda again and just had his timing be better.

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:10.300
But they even implement new moves for him and stuff, which is cool.

00:13:11.460 --> 00:13:15.220
Yeah. And then there's that limited move set for Little Mac.

00:13:15.420 --> 00:13:19.720
You know, he's got jabs, body blows, the uppercut, and then that star special.

00:13:20.540 --> 00:13:25.380
Doesn't sound like a lot, but it really works effectively just giving you that limited range.

00:13:25.560 --> 00:13:37.520
I think that the simplicity of it allows for a lot of creativity in how you go about creating your combos or just discovering what works on what character.

00:13:37.520 --> 00:13:44.640
Yeah, because it doesn't feel like when you start, it doesn't feel like a game that you would have to use a lot of strategy or really thinking about it.

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:47.980
You would think you're just trying to hit him as much as you can.

00:13:48.080 --> 00:13:52.440
But it's really a lot of learning and strategizing for each opponent.

00:13:52.620 --> 00:13:54.000
It's almost like a puzzle game.

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:55.240
Exactly what I was going to say.

00:13:55.360 --> 00:13:58.820
Yeah. This game is a puzzle game masquerading as a sports title.

00:13:59.120 --> 00:14:02.920
Yeah. Tricked you. And the best, Sam and I have both followed the speed running.

00:14:02.920 --> 00:14:08.180
And the best players in the world, for them, it's now, it's still like a dexterity battle.

00:14:08.380 --> 00:14:14.260
Like, it's getting the inputs in fast enough so that they can shave off the slightest milliseconds off their time.

00:14:14.480 --> 00:14:20.440
Because if they can just get that punch in a beat earlier, because the game actually is timed to the millisecond.

00:14:20.640 --> 00:14:27.280
So if they can get that punch in just a millisecond earlier, it'll count and it'll get a different result than whatever would have happened next.

00:14:27.480 --> 00:14:29.960
Wow. There's very little RNG in the game.

00:14:30.160 --> 00:14:32.440
I think certain enemies have some RNG behind them.

00:14:32.440 --> 00:14:34.040
Mike Tyson has a lot in this fight.

00:14:34.420 --> 00:14:35.400
So there's that.

00:14:35.460 --> 00:14:37.920
But at the same time, there's the tells.

00:14:38.240 --> 00:14:41.040
And the tells are the most interesting part of the game for me.

00:14:41.460 --> 00:14:45.180
Every character has their own tell that they're about to punch you.

00:14:45.380 --> 00:14:48.120
Which is great because their punches do an awful lot of damage.

00:14:48.560 --> 00:14:50.300
So it's much better to know.

00:14:50.520 --> 00:14:52.360
I think of this in a shmup, right?

00:14:52.580 --> 00:15:01.180
Wouldn't it be great if every ship that you were seeing ahead of you communicated that it was about to charge at you or shoot at you rather than just act randomly?

00:15:01.600 --> 00:15:05.440
And that's kind of what these fighters do is they communicate, I'm about to punch you.

00:15:05.580 --> 00:15:07.300
And you think like, oh, that sounds really easy.

00:15:07.360 --> 00:15:08.720
He's going to tell me every single time.

00:15:08.860 --> 00:15:10.880
But you have to be in rhythm with him.

00:15:10.980 --> 00:15:13.600
So it's as much a puzzle game as it is a rhythm game.

00:15:13.820 --> 00:15:18.660
You're right. Any other thoughts on that timing and telling mechanic?

00:15:18.900 --> 00:15:20.620
Because I think the tell is something that we...

00:15:20.620 --> 00:15:22.200
Well, that is the defining...

00:15:22.200 --> 00:15:31.100
That is what separates each level of the game is just what pattern they have, what tell you'll have to...

00:15:31.100 --> 00:15:32.300
They communicate to you.

00:15:33.020 --> 00:15:36.940
So without them, this is just a series of the same match over and over.

00:15:37.060 --> 00:15:40.200
So yeah, I think it's the most important part of the fighting mechanics.

00:15:40.340 --> 00:15:44.580
It reminds me a little bit of something like Monster Hunter.

00:15:45.140 --> 00:15:48.420
Oh, yeah. You know, where the thing that you're trying to kill has...

00:15:49.200 --> 00:15:55.040
You know, it has a certain tell to it that you have to figure out and exploit and figure out how to get around that thing.

00:15:55.160 --> 00:15:58.320
And even when you think you've figured it out, a new one gets added.

00:15:58.320 --> 00:16:04.320
So there's this whole thing of when you think you've figured out Piston Honda, the champion for the first circuit,

00:16:04.560 --> 00:16:09.020
all of a sudden he takes a step back and he starts charging up for something.

00:16:09.240 --> 00:16:11.080
And, you know, I've never seen this before.

00:16:11.120 --> 00:16:12.000
What's about to happen?

00:16:12.060 --> 00:16:14.120
And then he goes left, right, left, right, left, right.

00:16:14.580 --> 00:16:17.140
And there's many strategies to how to work around that.

00:16:17.420 --> 00:16:22.980
Sam showed me a really cool one right before we started the show where you give him a belly punch at the perfect time to knock him out.

00:16:23.040 --> 00:16:23.980
And he goes, oh, my tummy.

00:16:24.280 --> 00:16:25.800
He does. He really does say that.

00:16:25.800 --> 00:16:27.840
He says that verbatim, yeah.

00:16:28.700 --> 00:16:29.720
Uh, no. No.

00:16:31.380 --> 00:16:33.300
What's funny is we have three different stories here.

00:16:33.360 --> 00:16:34.360
He says it in English.

00:16:34.380 --> 00:16:35.660
He says it in Japanese.

00:16:35.880 --> 00:16:36.700
He says it at all.

00:16:37.620 --> 00:16:40.000
I know the answer, but I'm just not saying it.

00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:43.380
Okay, good. I was just so excited to perform it that I wasn't listening to it.

00:16:43.520 --> 00:16:47.640
That's cool. I mean, there is some voice synthesis in this game, right?

00:16:48.820 --> 00:16:53.200
I don't have a recollection of any actual voice synthesis.

00:16:53.540 --> 00:17:24.780
Okay. I wasn't approved that he was Mario.

00:17:24.780 --> 00:17:28.780
You think it's just another, like, generic short Italian man?

00:17:28.920 --> 00:17:30.200
Well, he shows up in everything.

00:17:30.500 --> 00:17:32.780
He was the golfer in golf.

00:17:33.340 --> 00:17:35.840
I, that one's still a little bit of a stretch for me, the golfer.

00:17:35.840 --> 00:17:36.880
And it turns out not to be real.

00:17:37.020 --> 00:17:42.020
Remember we talked about that obscure Japanese Wii game that gives that golf character a totally different person.

00:17:42.020 --> 00:17:44.220
You're telling me right now that Mario is not the referee.

00:17:44.400 --> 00:17:47.060
No, I'm sure he is, but he was also the umpire in tennis.

00:17:47.220 --> 00:17:49.880
So how many times does this guy have between saving the princess?

00:17:49.880 --> 00:17:55.780
The thing about Mario is he has all the time in the world between saving the princess times.

00:17:56.020 --> 00:17:57.420
Yeah, what is he? He's like, he's kind of waiting around.

00:17:57.420 --> 00:17:58.460
Is he only saving princess?

00:17:58.460 --> 00:17:59.780
He's a prover. These are his weekends.

00:18:00.040 --> 00:18:03.140
Yeah. Well, it is in New York City, so at least that makes sense.

00:18:03.400 --> 00:18:06.520
You know, there's no, I mean, maybe he was playing at the U.S.

00:18:06.620 --> 00:18:08.800
Open for the tennis thing, and that's why he was the umpire.

00:18:09.560 --> 00:18:13.300
But to Sam's point, you mentioned that he was kind of snuck into the game.

00:18:13.420 --> 00:18:14.580
I heard that somewhere. Is that true?

00:18:14.580 --> 00:18:23.940
I believe it just because Miyamoto had this thing where whenever there was like a chance to throw in Mario in the early days, he would just be like, oh, well, we can make Mario that, or we can put Mario here.

00:18:24.140 --> 00:18:29.180
There was a whole thing for Super Mario Brothers where that game was originally just like.

00:18:29.180 --> 00:18:30.900
Not going to have Mario. Yeah, seriously.

00:18:31.260 --> 00:18:34.040
Wait, seriously? Yeah, they didn't know like what they were going to do with it.

00:18:34.080 --> 00:18:39.200
They were just designing this big game, and then they were like, well, Mario Brothers is selling really well.

00:18:39.400 --> 00:18:40.980
Let's put Super Mario Brothers.

00:18:40.980 --> 00:18:47.100
For some reason, I was imagining like it was for Mario Brothers 2, and I was like, should we even put Mario in there?

00:18:47.880 --> 00:18:48.860
Mario is missing.

00:18:49.220 --> 00:19:14.420
Yeah. So I think Punch-Out!

00:19:14.460 --> 00:19:17.080
worked best by what it doesn't let you control.

00:19:17.440 --> 00:19:18.700
Does that make sense?

00:19:18.920 --> 00:19:21.280
Yeah. Like, you can't control his movement.

00:19:21.460 --> 00:19:23.480
Yeah, and remember Ring King?

00:19:23.900 --> 00:19:25.820
I don't know if it was just a human meat thing.

00:19:25.960 --> 00:19:26.840
The classic Ring King?

00:19:26.960 --> 00:19:28.760
Yeah, the BJ thing.

00:19:29.080 --> 00:19:29.960
Oh. Is that the BJ game?

00:19:29.980 --> 00:19:31.520
Well, yeah, that was in that.

00:19:31.680 --> 00:19:33.400
Yeah. Defining characteristic of the game.

00:19:33.740 --> 00:19:36.020
Yeah. What does that say about that game?

00:19:36.120 --> 00:19:37.440
That's the first thing you think of.

00:19:37.640 --> 00:19:39.680
But anyway, Ring King, that was a boxing game.

00:19:39.680 --> 00:19:43.080
Yeah. And you get to do everything you could possibly do in boxing.

00:19:43.340 --> 00:19:44.820
You pivot around. You can clinch.

00:19:45.400 --> 00:19:48.700
And I don't know about you, Sean, but for me, that made a very clunky experience.

00:19:49.040 --> 00:19:50.620
It was a terrible game. Yeah, that didn't do anything.

00:19:50.820 --> 00:19:55.640
Whereas in this one, because they've decided you shouldn't have to move, you could duck out

00:19:55.640 --> 00:19:59.380
of the way, but you shouldn't have to move, now all of a sudden you have to focus more

00:19:59.380 --> 00:20:04.640
on the punches. And because you only have two different types of punches and then left and right choosing

00:20:04.640 --> 00:20:08.100
your sides, now you don't have many ways to control your punch, so you don't have to

00:20:08.100 --> 00:20:09.900
worry about not connecting those punches.

00:20:10.040 --> 00:20:11.700
It's either going to be blocked or it's going to punch.

00:20:11.920 --> 00:20:16.300
Right. I think the limited thing here, the simplicity of it, is actually what complements it the

00:20:16.300 --> 00:20:18.580
most. Yeah. I think that works in a lot of games.

00:20:18.920 --> 00:20:22.720
Go ahead. I think in developing it, it made it a lot easier for them.

00:20:22.780 --> 00:20:27.300
I imagine it would make it a lot easier for them to create something where they can control

00:20:27.300 --> 00:20:30.820
what strategy you're supposed to be trying to figure out.

00:20:30.920 --> 00:20:33.060
So they're figuring out, this is what we need them to do.

00:20:33.120 --> 00:20:36.180
We need them to think to hit this weak spot and that weak spot.

00:20:36.180 --> 00:20:39.340
But if you have too many things that you're able to do where you're thinking, I'll just

00:20:39.340 --> 00:20:45.360
run to this side of the ring or dodge this way, it kind of lends itself to having specific

00:20:45.360 --> 00:20:47.120
strategies that you're supposed to discover.

00:20:47.260 --> 00:20:54.240
For sure. I think it also is just another example of the prevailing theme so far in the NES catalog

00:20:54.240 --> 00:20:59.260
of simulation sports games really having a hard time being good.

00:20:59.340 --> 00:21:05.220
And when they actually go in a more arcade route, you can have a lot more fun with it because

00:21:05.220 --> 00:21:08.540
you don't have to worry about micromanaging all the simulation aspects of it.

00:21:08.940 --> 00:21:14.040
Yeah. I mean, imagine if all of a sudden, let's just say you could hold on to your opponent.

00:21:14.240 --> 00:21:17.040
You know, in boxing, sometimes they'll put their arms on the other guy's shoulder.

00:21:17.260 --> 00:21:18.680
Imagine if that was like an option.

00:21:18.840 --> 00:21:20.800
Forget that there's not an input for that, right?

00:21:20.880 --> 00:21:22.220
Like you wouldn't even know how to map that.

00:21:22.360 --> 00:21:26.240
But that's a whole nother thing now that you have to add to the game for each character

00:21:26.240 --> 00:21:27.460
to work a separate way.

00:21:27.580 --> 00:21:30.860
And so by doing that, you're just complicating the game further than it needs to be.

00:21:30.980 --> 00:21:35.140
I also think even just imagine if you could punch the chest instead of just the

00:21:35.140 --> 00:21:40.360
body or the head, you know, it'd be nice, but you don't have to have it either because

00:21:40.360 --> 00:21:41.800
it's perfectly fine without it.

00:21:42.000 --> 00:21:43.360
I think simplicity is king in this game.

00:21:43.560 --> 00:21:46.100
Yeah. I think in every game, honestly.

00:21:46.380 --> 00:21:48.240
Oh, okay. Yeah. Especially for these older games.

00:21:48.300 --> 00:21:52.920
I agree with that. Especially when, yeah, when the limitations of the system are only so much.

00:21:52.920 --> 00:21:54.660
So you work well within that.

00:21:54.860 --> 00:21:58.760
One of my favorite games on the system so far, Balloon Fight, is one of the most simple

00:21:58.760 --> 00:21:59.880
games that we've ever played.

00:22:00.140 --> 00:22:02.220
Yeah. Anybody can pick that game up.

00:22:03.020 --> 00:22:05.880
And I don't even think you really need instructions.

00:22:06.420 --> 00:22:07.900
No. You just start pressing the button.

00:22:08.240 --> 00:22:12.600
So speaking of instructions, I think that's going to get us into Glass Joe, who we can finally

00:22:12.600 --> 00:22:14.000
talk about. Not Joe over here.

00:22:14.620 --> 00:22:18.700
Glass Joe is the first enemy you fight in the minor circuit.

00:22:19.220 --> 00:22:20.940
And he's not really a tutorial.

00:22:21.220 --> 00:22:24.280
You know, we've seen things where Super Mario Brothers has like that.

00:22:24.440 --> 00:22:26.520
He's the closest thing this game gets to one.

00:22:26.520 --> 00:22:33.120
Yeah, exactly. But there's no teaching you via pop-up commands or anything like that what you need to do.

00:22:33.260 --> 00:22:36.320
He just lets you punch him no matter what.

00:22:36.480 --> 00:22:39.760
Like you must punch him first before he ever starts doing anything to you.

00:22:39.840 --> 00:22:41.740
That's true. And I think that's a smart thing to do.

00:22:41.840 --> 00:22:44.780
It's like if you want to connect, if you want to learn how to play this game, you got

00:22:44.780 --> 00:22:46.060
to connect to punch. Mm-hmm.

00:22:46.580 --> 00:22:48.900
I didn't, yeah, I guess I did notice that.

00:22:49.400 --> 00:22:55.120
I thought it was just an AI thing where you had to, you had to make a move and he would

00:22:55.120 --> 00:22:57.300
just like try to counter it at first.

00:22:57.420 --> 00:22:59.920
But no, it makes sense that, yeah, you have to land a blow.

00:23:00.640 --> 00:23:03.160
Yeah. Any other thoughts on Glass Joe at all?

00:23:03.280 --> 00:23:05.360
I mean, he's, he's funny, I think.

00:23:05.700 --> 00:23:06.820
What a poor name.

00:23:07.060 --> 00:23:12.160
Yeah. Imagine if you're being introduced into your boxing, not your debut.

00:23:12.360 --> 00:23:13.500
Like you've been around for a while.

00:23:13.880 --> 00:23:14.700
A long, long time. Yeah.

00:23:15.020 --> 00:23:18.440
Oh, I mean, there's Glass, there's like a WWE character.

00:23:18.800 --> 00:23:19.960
Yeah, there's Glass Mike.

00:23:20.360 --> 00:23:22.440
I wonder how he's going to do today.

00:23:22.440 --> 00:23:30.220
Aside from Mike Tyson himself, I think he is the closest thing to another icon in the

00:23:31.900 --> 00:23:33.580
mythos of Punch-Out.

00:23:33.760 --> 00:23:37.740
Like Glass Joe is probably the most famous character outside of Little Mac.

00:23:37.740 --> 00:23:40.360
Because anybody who's ever played Punch-Out has played against Glass Joe.

00:23:40.680 --> 00:23:41.760
He always goes first.

00:23:41.920 --> 00:23:43.440
I believe he's in Super Punch-Out, right?

00:23:43.440 --> 00:23:44.840
He is. He's the first person you play against.

00:23:44.860 --> 00:23:45.760
And he was in Punch-Out, the arcade.

00:23:45.860 --> 00:23:46.840
He's the first person you play against.

00:23:46.860 --> 00:23:48.840
Poor guy's getting beat up for his whole life.

00:23:49.040 --> 00:23:53.400
Yeah. And it's kind of those in-between rounds things, which we haven't really talked about yet.

00:23:53.400 --> 00:24:00.300
But in-between rounds, you have like the corner and you hear Doc Lewis talking to you and

00:24:00.300 --> 00:24:03.300
the other enemy kind of giving like a hint.

00:24:03.540 --> 00:24:05.500
Yeah. So like how to fight them.

00:24:05.780 --> 00:24:07.100
And I like that.

00:24:07.200 --> 00:24:09.580
But with Glass Joe, there's no hint.

00:24:09.880 --> 00:24:13.880
It's just like, please, let's just get this over with.

00:24:14.420 --> 00:24:16.540
Can I take a nap before the next round?

00:24:16.580 --> 00:24:17.640
I think is one of his lines.

00:24:18.480 --> 00:24:19.800
So poor Glass Joe.

00:24:20.220 --> 00:24:21.300
Thankfully, he's pretty easy.

00:24:21.500 --> 00:24:24.260
So I don't mean that it's not a tutorial in a bad way.

00:24:24.260 --> 00:24:28.260
I mean, it's nice that they didn't feel the need to explain it to you, but made it easy

00:24:28.260 --> 00:24:31.940
enough that you can just kind of learn the ropes right there for the entirety of the game.

00:24:32.120 --> 00:24:34.740
Every mechanic that you need is presented in there.

00:24:35.060 --> 00:24:44.920
Yeah. And one thing we haven't talked about yet is the accumulation of stars.

00:24:45.460 --> 00:24:47.240
Does anybody want to try to tackle that?

00:24:47.800 --> 00:24:48.700
So I got this.

00:24:48.880 --> 00:24:55.300
So the way that stars work are when you press start, you get a and you have it when you have

00:24:55.300 --> 00:25:02.260
a star, you press start to do a special uppercut that does a ton of damage and it's good for

00:25:02.260 --> 00:25:09.400
knocking people out. And the way you get those stars is by hitting people in certain ways.

00:25:09.960 --> 00:25:16.300
I think that one of them is if you get enough uppercuts against, I forget which person.

00:25:16.420 --> 00:25:17.880
It's unique to each character.

00:25:18.040 --> 00:25:19.620
And this is something Sean and I were talking about.

00:25:19.780 --> 00:25:20.900
And it's hard to figure out.

00:25:21.060 --> 00:25:24.120
You know, you really need to look it up, but it's also something you just experience on

00:25:24.120 --> 00:25:28.520
your own too. And later in the game, they become essential and you actually need them to beat the

00:25:28.520 --> 00:25:30.000
characters. Yeah.

00:25:30.160 --> 00:25:32.740
And I think that the uppercut inclusion is nice.

00:25:32.740 --> 00:25:37.080
It's nice that it's mapped to the start button, a button that you can't otherwise do anything

00:25:37.080 --> 00:25:42.000
with because much to probably Joe's disappointment, there's no pause sound in this game because

00:25:42.000 --> 00:25:43.180
you cannot pause.

00:25:43.860 --> 00:25:45.900
You know, I don't think I tried to pause the game.

00:25:46.220 --> 00:25:48.980
It doesn't seem like you should pause, but I'm having a great time.

00:25:49.000 --> 00:25:50.140
Who pauses in the middle of a boxing match?

00:25:50.420 --> 00:25:54.740
You should think that I'm going to be the one that's going to try and pause every game.

00:25:54.740 --> 00:25:58.500
So I apologize. This is how winter games should have handled pausing.

00:25:58.720 --> 00:25:59.580
Do you remember winter games?

00:25:59.740 --> 00:26:01.760
Yeah. The whole down start.

00:26:02.360 --> 00:26:05.920
And then you, you know, like if you let go of it, it just resumed.

00:26:06.180 --> 00:26:07.740
It should have just been no pausing at all.

00:26:08.080 --> 00:26:10.780
You can't pause in the middle of your events and your sport.

00:26:11.060 --> 00:26:11.980
Really? All games.

00:26:12.180 --> 00:26:13.260
Don't make sense. All games should.

00:26:13.260 --> 00:26:15.360
Yeah. It doesn't make sense. I just had a random thought with winter games.

00:26:15.500 --> 00:26:17.600
What if you hold it and then unplug the controller?

00:26:17.820 --> 00:26:18.760
Do you think that would work? Wow.

00:26:18.760 --> 00:26:19.920
We should try that. Yeah.

00:26:20.100 --> 00:26:21.200
Okay. I'll try that. No, I'm serious.

00:26:21.300 --> 00:26:22.260
I'll try that. That's a great thought.

00:26:22.620 --> 00:26:26.080
I'm sure it won't work. I'm sure it will just assume that you've let go of Stark.

00:26:26.700 --> 00:26:28.140
This podcast is over.

00:26:28.320 --> 00:26:31.900
See us next week after we've tried the removing of the controller.

00:26:32.060 --> 00:26:33.800
Please switch to side B on the whole set.

00:26:33.840 --> 00:26:36.620
Full episode of what happens when you remove the controller.

00:26:36.900 --> 00:26:37.820
So that's the stars.

00:26:37.880 --> 00:26:39.120
And then there's hearts, too.

00:26:39.280 --> 00:26:40.680
Anybody want to try and give hearts a go?

00:26:41.520 --> 00:26:43.860
Hearts is basically your stamina.

00:26:44.880 --> 00:26:49.280
Yeah. Every time you miss, you'll lose hearts.

00:26:49.440 --> 00:26:50.980
Every time you get hit, you'll lose hearts.

00:26:51.020 --> 00:26:54.580
It's the well from which you pull your energy to do anything.

00:26:54.720 --> 00:26:56.960
And once you're out of hearts, you turn pink, right?

00:26:57.040 --> 00:26:57.900
You're like glow pink.

00:26:58.000 --> 00:26:59.120
You are very tired.

00:26:59.360 --> 00:27:00.440
And you're tired. And you cannot attack.

00:27:00.440 --> 00:27:01.700
You can only dodge. Yeah.

00:27:01.900 --> 00:27:06.560
And you have to dodge for essentially five seconds before you can start punching again.

00:27:06.760 --> 00:27:13.300
I think it's a great mechanic, though, because it also allows for rallies to happen.

00:27:13.300 --> 00:27:20.240
Like, you see the guy getting hit in the boxing match in real life, and he can either just

00:27:20.240 --> 00:27:26.500
go down or he'll weather the storm for a while and come back with a storm of his own.

00:27:26.580 --> 00:27:30.660
It's a great punishment, too, to not just keep trying body check after body check.

00:27:30.840 --> 00:27:32.280
It forces you to be decisive.

00:27:32.500 --> 00:27:34.940
Even the blocks count against your stamina.

00:27:35.080 --> 00:27:38.960
If you keep punching him in the stomach and Glass Joe keeps blocking it, you're actually

00:27:38.960 --> 00:27:43.240
losing stamina by doing that, because you would get winded in a real boxing match if you just

00:27:43.240 --> 00:27:46.260
kept throwing your right arm again and again and again.

00:27:46.460 --> 00:27:49.000
So the one system accomplishes multiple things.

00:27:49.180 --> 00:27:51.180
Yeah. That's great. And did anybody else...

00:27:51.180 --> 00:27:54.320
I kind of skipped over this real fast, but the intermission between rounds.

00:27:54.420 --> 00:27:56.100
Did anybody have any other thoughts on that?

00:27:56.240 --> 00:28:00.400
I think it's a pretty cool way to handle the only time you can pause.

00:28:00.620 --> 00:28:02.260
I like how it changes depending on how you're doing.

00:28:02.520 --> 00:28:08.740
Yeah. If you get beat up a lot, then Little Mac looks like shit, and his eye is swollen, and he's

00:28:08.740 --> 00:28:10.180
got a little purple around it, too.

00:28:10.440 --> 00:28:11.560
He's the same with the opposition, too.

00:28:11.620 --> 00:28:13.620
And he says, I don't know how much longer I can take it.

00:28:13.700 --> 00:28:18.440
Yeah. And then the opposition, yeah, the opposition, you see how they're doing as well.

00:28:18.720 --> 00:28:22.080
And sometimes they get angry, and sometimes they're smug.

00:28:22.540 --> 00:28:23.800
So I think it's really cool.

00:28:24.040 --> 00:28:29.540
One thing I talked to Sam about, and I read this, so I don't know how much it's confirmed,

00:28:29.540 --> 00:28:36.980
but if you press select as soon as the round goes to the cutscene, when you resume the

00:28:36.980 --> 00:28:41.620
next fight, you gain a ton more energy, or health, essentially.

00:28:42.240 --> 00:28:47.120
You gain a ton more health back, because that's kind of like helping apply the bandages.

00:28:47.560 --> 00:28:48.860
Yeah, that is in the manual.

00:28:48.980 --> 00:28:51.320
Oh, okay, great. So it is an actual thing.

00:28:51.560 --> 00:28:54.840
Yeah. I think a lot of this game already is showing...

00:28:54.840 --> 00:28:59.020
We're only on Glass Joe, but it's showing a lot of promise because of all the little

00:28:59.020 --> 00:29:00.380
details that it puts into it.

00:29:01.160 --> 00:29:06.640
So after Glass Joe, you face Von Kaiser, and I don't really have much to say about him,

00:29:06.680 --> 00:29:11.500
other than he was the one where I learned my essential strategy for most of the enemies

00:29:11.500 --> 00:29:16.680
in the game, which is after you knock down Von Kaiser the first time, he will most likely

00:29:16.680 --> 00:29:20.200
get up. It'll be one, two, three, and then he'll get back up.

00:29:20.500 --> 00:29:26.820
And then if you dodge his attack, whatever comes next, dodge it, hit him with an uppercut,

00:29:26.820 --> 00:29:31.060
you can switch between uppercut left, uppercut right, uppercut left, uppercut right.

00:29:31.220 --> 00:29:32.860
You mean like a hook left or right?

00:29:32.860 --> 00:29:34.100
Oh, sorry. Yeah, hook left, hook right.

00:29:34.240 --> 00:29:39.480
Yeah, like that. And you can just continue to chain it again and again, as long as you alternate between

00:29:39.480 --> 00:29:40.640
left, right, left, right.

00:29:40.980 --> 00:29:42.460
He'll never break out of it.

00:29:42.700 --> 00:29:46.620
Now, again, he must be knocked down once before this happens, but he'll never break out

00:29:46.620 --> 00:29:48.460
of it and he'll go right to being knocked down again.

00:29:48.660 --> 00:29:52.380
It's not much fun, but if I'm playing through this game from the beginning again and I want to

00:29:52.380 --> 00:29:54.840
get through it pretty fast, you better believe I'm going to use that strategy.

00:29:54.840 --> 00:29:57.320
It also feels pretty awesome the first time you do it.

00:29:57.460 --> 00:29:58.640
Yeah, so it gets me pumped.

00:29:58.820 --> 00:30:01.540
Yeah. So that's Von Kaiser.

00:30:01.540 --> 00:30:02.480
Any other thoughts on him?

00:30:03.080 --> 00:30:08.360
Okay. And then Piston Honda, a guy we've been talking about a lot because he is the first like massive

00:30:08.800 --> 00:30:09.880
character you fight.

00:30:10.480 --> 00:30:12.840
Kind of looks like the guy from Street Fighter.

00:30:13.440 --> 00:30:14.520
Which guy from Street Fighter?

00:30:14.700 --> 00:30:16.260
Oh, yeah, Ryu?

00:30:16.700 --> 00:30:19.880
No. Maybe I'm thinking of a different fighting game.

00:30:19.880 --> 00:30:21.620
I think you are thinking of a different fighting game.

00:30:22.200 --> 00:30:24.720
Okay. Yeah. I'll just go back into my corner.

00:30:24.720 --> 00:30:27.280
I was like, yeah, because I don't think he really looks like Ryu, but I was trying to

00:30:27.280 --> 00:30:29.300
throw you a bone. Sam?

00:30:29.740 --> 00:30:34.140
This was the first one that when I was playing this the first time, it kind of felt like a

00:30:34.140 --> 00:30:37.520
real fight where the other ones you can kind of cheese your way out of it.

00:30:37.940 --> 00:30:44.520
This one really, I think this is where the game actually starts and stops being a tutorial

00:30:44.520 --> 00:30:48.560
and starts being more of a game because he's got his little special fight that he does.

00:30:48.560 --> 00:30:50.740
Yeah, the back up and then approach.

00:30:50.820 --> 00:30:53.820
He backs up and then he charges and he has the Fists of Fury or whatever it is.

00:30:53.940 --> 00:30:55.800
So what was it when that first happened to you guys?

00:30:55.860 --> 00:30:58.680
Because I think that's the first like trick move in the game, right?

00:30:58.760 --> 00:31:01.680
Yeah. When that came out, what was your first strategy?

00:31:01.860 --> 00:31:03.660
Don't tell me what you actually have to do.

00:31:03.760 --> 00:31:05.420
But what was your first reaction to that?

00:31:05.580 --> 00:31:06.760
Did you just take the blows?

00:31:06.980 --> 00:31:08.840
Did you try to dodge to the left and right?

00:31:08.840 --> 00:31:10.040
I think I tried to dodge.

00:31:10.360 --> 00:31:11.880
Yeah, I tried to dodge too. I didn't do it very well.

00:31:12.080 --> 00:31:15.380
Yeah. It was tough because I mean that was especially the first time I saw that coming.

00:31:16.180 --> 00:31:17.760
I had no idea that was coming.

00:31:17.800 --> 00:31:20.080
So it just kind of took me by surprise.

00:31:20.280 --> 00:31:25.560
I think I tried to do a dodge punch like I tried with the other moves and that worked.

00:31:25.960 --> 00:31:31.000
Yeah. I wonder, has anybody been able to dodge left or right and successfully time it?

00:31:31.100 --> 00:31:35.020
Or will you be by dodging to the left or right, will you eventually get hit by one of his punches?

00:31:35.200 --> 00:31:36.620
Like can you perfectly time those?

00:31:36.620 --> 00:31:39.840
Because I know if you hit down on the deep head, you'll block.

00:31:40.000 --> 00:31:45.380
I think that you can get into a good enough rhythm so that you can be missed by all of them.

00:31:45.520 --> 00:31:48.680
You can also, if you hit down twice, I think you dodge as well.

00:31:48.680 --> 00:31:50.700
Oh, really? Okay. I did not notice that either.

00:31:50.840 --> 00:31:52.180
I really need to check out this manual.

00:31:52.480 --> 00:31:53.640
I think that's true.

00:31:53.840 --> 00:31:57.140
Okay. But I never dodged it successfully.

00:31:57.380 --> 00:32:01.000
All that said though for Piston Honda, he's still an approachable character.

00:32:01.220 --> 00:32:03.240
The game hasn't hit like hard mode yet.

00:32:03.240 --> 00:32:05.920
It's just giving you something new that you have to prepare for.

00:32:05.920 --> 00:32:13.960
I think it was, like Sam said, I think it was the first time for me where I really had to think of a strategy or kind of decide, be more decisive with my attacks.

00:32:14.060 --> 00:32:21.220
Like when he does some crazy uppercut and that's when you got to jump in and, you know, throw all your attacks at once.

00:32:21.400 --> 00:32:24.380
Right. And we mentioned a little, the quick way to get in.

00:32:24.380 --> 00:32:25.320
All-time body punch.

00:32:26.320 --> 00:32:29.000
Yeah. And kudos to Sam for showing that to me.

00:32:29.240 --> 00:32:32.020
So then you become the champion of the minor circuit.

00:32:32.020 --> 00:32:34.100
There's a nice little screen for that and everything.

00:32:34.320 --> 00:32:38.740
And then there's the amazing cut scene that we just talked about where he's in Staten Island?

00:32:38.900 --> 00:32:39.940
Staten Island. Let's go with it.

00:32:40.060 --> 00:32:42.640
Maybe a lower, maybe West Village.

00:32:42.640 --> 00:32:49.620
And it was interesting, too, because I, really my only knowledge of Little Mac was from my also limited knowledge of Super Smash Brothers.

00:32:49.840 --> 00:32:52.680
And there's a skin in Super Smash Brothers where he's wearing a pink hoodie.

00:32:52.780 --> 00:32:54.640
And I always thought it was just a funny thing they put in there.

00:32:54.700 --> 00:32:55.880
But it's from that cut scene.

00:32:56.100 --> 00:32:58.940
Well, then you'll also know in Smash Brothers there's a wireframe version, too.

00:32:59.300 --> 00:33:01.240
Yeah. And that's from the arcade version.

00:33:02.720 --> 00:33:04.620
You just pointed that out to me, just so you know.

00:33:04.620 --> 00:33:09.160
I did not put the connection between the pink hoodie and the Smash Brothers skin.

00:33:09.400 --> 00:33:10.440
So that's really cool.

00:33:40.120 --> 00:33:47.340
I did not put the connection between the two and the two and the two. After the amazing cut scene, you go to the major circuit, which obviously would be a little step up from the minor circuit.

00:33:47.480 --> 00:33:50.460
But you're not quite up to Mike Tyson's scale yet.

00:33:50.640 --> 00:33:55.820
And that's when you get, in my opinion, the real personality of the game starts to come out.

00:33:55.900 --> 00:34:00.260
The first three characters definitely feel like people you might run into in a boxing match.

00:34:00.540 --> 00:34:02.440
You know, they're real people.

00:34:02.620 --> 00:34:04.840
Then Don Flamenco comes out.

00:34:05.180 --> 00:34:09.800
And that music, you know, which I imagine is just royalty-free music, right?

00:34:09.800 --> 00:34:12.000
What is that? I forget what song it is.

00:34:12.160 --> 00:34:17.420
Yeah. Anyway, he comes out to that with a rose.

00:34:17.740 --> 00:34:19.900
Spanish-sounding, yeah. It's a famous song.

00:34:20.020 --> 00:34:22.540
I can't remember. Yeah. It's just so funny that he's doing this.

00:34:23.160 --> 00:34:27.140
And that kind of, like, put a smile on my face coming off of the cut scene.

00:34:27.200 --> 00:34:28.640
You know, I'm already like, let's do it.

00:34:28.740 --> 00:34:30.020
Next round. And then you see this.

00:34:30.020 --> 00:34:31.320
And I'm like, I can fight this guy.

00:34:32.260 --> 00:34:33.700
Like, he's better than Glass Joe.

00:34:34.100 --> 00:34:35.180
Kind of looks like Glass Joe.

00:34:35.580 --> 00:34:36.600
A little? A little bit.

00:34:36.600 --> 00:34:37.680
Okay. He's tanned.

00:34:37.900 --> 00:34:40.540
We're having a hard time, like, visualizing things right now.

00:34:40.740 --> 00:34:42.200
Yeah. I was seeing...

00:34:42.200 --> 00:34:43.340
Yeah, he had black hair.

00:34:43.480 --> 00:34:45.800
I was seeing Honda from Street Fighter.

00:34:46.120 --> 00:34:47.560
I think a big part of it is...

00:34:47.560 --> 00:34:51.120
You were seeing Honda? I think a big part of it is just that he's...

00:34:51.440 --> 00:34:53.900
Since Glass Joe, we've seen a lot of people that are, like, twice your size.

00:34:54.000 --> 00:34:55.120
This is the other guy that's, like, more your size.

00:34:55.260 --> 00:34:56.560
So it reminded me of him. He's a skinny guy.

00:34:56.740 --> 00:34:58.800
Probably not 107 pounds like yourself.

00:34:59.660 --> 00:35:01.940
But, nevertheless, he's a pro 17-year-old.

00:35:01.940 --> 00:35:06.460
But, yeah, flamenco sort of gets by just by taunting you the whole time.

00:35:06.660 --> 00:35:08.720
Yeah. So that's new.

00:35:09.200 --> 00:35:10.560
I like that, though, too.

00:35:10.640 --> 00:35:15.460
The idea of a cocky fighter who thinks, like, oh, you're from the minor circuit.

00:35:15.460 --> 00:35:16.460
I'm on the major circuit.

00:35:16.480 --> 00:35:18.640
Your hustle is 17 and 107 pounds.

00:35:19.480 --> 00:35:21.720
Right. This is probably going to be pretty easy.

00:35:21.820 --> 00:35:24.420
I solved flamenco with the old left and right again.

00:35:24.960 --> 00:35:26.400
Just doing the knockdown.

00:35:26.560 --> 00:35:27.860
And then when he gets back up, doing that.

00:35:27.860 --> 00:35:33.200
But there are people who play this game that get very creative with knocking people out as fast as possible.

00:35:33.680 --> 00:35:40.800
And one thing that's really interesting with the speedrunning scene is that they don't time the game from real-time duration.

00:35:41.200 --> 00:35:43.200
They just go from fight to fight.

00:35:43.300 --> 00:35:51.580
And then at the end of each fight, they add up the total time because it tells you, like, you beat him in 2 minutes, 13 seconds, .2.

00:35:51.820 --> 00:35:52.640
You know? Interesting.

00:35:52.840 --> 00:35:55.360
And they add up each of those to get a final thing.

00:35:55.360 --> 00:36:00.260
Just because, like, the time between rounds is set?

00:36:00.600 --> 00:36:03.500
I guess it's just more like, oh, how fast can you hit the start button?

00:36:03.780 --> 00:36:06.840
You know? Yeah. Making sure that, you know, there's no skill really involved there.

00:36:06.840 --> 00:36:09.560
So I guess there was more just making sure that the fights count.

00:36:09.840 --> 00:36:20.500
Yeah. But it's people figuring out, you know, and this is not specifically for Don Flamenco, but there's people who figured out, oh, if you knock this guy out at exactly 43 seconds, it will just count as a KO.

00:36:20.500 --> 00:36:24.320
Like, he will not get up as long as you do the timer at 43 seconds.

00:36:24.660 --> 00:36:28.680
Or if you give this guy two star punches in a row, he'll TKO.

00:36:29.020 --> 00:36:33.020
You know? It's little things like that that people learn throughout it that I think is really interesting.

00:36:33.120 --> 00:36:34.520
Me, I'm just going at them.

00:36:34.660 --> 00:36:38.040
That 43-second thing seems like RNG, though.

00:36:38.280 --> 00:36:39.660
Not RNG because it's not random.

00:36:39.820 --> 00:36:43.300
It's 43 seconds. But what logic is behind that?

00:36:43.440 --> 00:36:44.420
I don't know. It's interesting.

00:36:44.600 --> 00:36:48.040
And who knows? For all, I think the 43 is the only thing that's required.

00:36:48.040 --> 00:36:50.220
I don't think it's a specific series of inputs.

00:36:50.660 --> 00:36:54.740
So, yeah, it seems like almost it was coded into the game to have that.

00:36:55.160 --> 00:36:57.700
Interesting. Any other thoughts on Flamenco?

00:36:58.540 --> 00:36:59.920
Who's not Flamenco?

00:37:01.100 --> 00:37:07.000
After that, we have King Hippo, who I would argue is the first trick fight of the game and maybe the only one.

00:37:07.640 --> 00:37:08.880
What do you mean by trick fight?

00:37:09.000 --> 00:37:14.580
So you can't actually do anything to him unless you, like, he must be KO'd.

00:37:14.860 --> 00:37:18.820
Yeah. And the only way to attack him is to knock down his pants.

00:37:20.000 --> 00:37:20.800
Oh, yes.

00:37:21.960 --> 00:37:22.980
Over and over again.

00:37:22.980 --> 00:37:23.980
Like, you can't, yeah, exactly.

00:37:24.140 --> 00:37:33.680
You can't hurt him through anything other than him winding up for his punch, which is different because he winds up for his punch sometimes and you can't do anything to him.

00:37:33.680 --> 00:37:42.620
But if he winds up his punch and opens his mouth, that's your chance to punch him in the belly, knock down his shorts, and then punch him in the belly as many times as possible.

00:37:42.620 --> 00:37:43.680
Everyone knows how shorts work.

00:37:43.920 --> 00:37:46.280
Your hands up, your mouth's open, your shorts are going to fall down.

00:37:46.440 --> 00:37:50.500
This fight was, I couldn't get past it for the longest time.

00:37:50.620 --> 00:37:59.560
Interesting. I had to look it up, but I didn't understand that I could only hit him if he raised his arm over his head with the mouth and stuff like that.

00:37:59.680 --> 00:38:00.780
Yeah. It was.

00:38:00.920 --> 00:38:02.880
He glows a little too on it though, too, right?

00:38:02.880 --> 00:38:04.500
He does like a brief shimmer.

00:38:04.880 --> 00:38:05.700
Maybe. Yeah.

00:38:05.700 --> 00:38:12.080
I know that in the cut scene though, in between rounds, he talks about his mouth or Doc Lewis talks about his mouth.

00:38:12.220 --> 00:38:13.040
I forget who does it.

00:38:13.280 --> 00:38:14.740
Well, first, Little Mac says something.

00:38:14.980 --> 00:38:19.260
This is all paraphrasing, but Little Mac says something along the lines of like, I just can't get through to him.

00:38:19.520 --> 00:38:22.900
And Doc gives like a borderline hint.

00:38:23.060 --> 00:38:26.420
But then Hippo is the one who talks about his mouth.

00:38:26.720 --> 00:38:29.460
And so that's like the key to like look for the mouth to be open.

00:38:29.460 --> 00:38:35.860
And it's also just, you have to be, I was so focused just staring at his face like, is he going to tell?

00:38:35.960 --> 00:38:45.060
Is he going to tell? And that visual information making its way to my hands of like whether I should punch or not just wasn't getting through.

00:38:45.460 --> 00:38:47.760
He's also got the bandage around his belly button.

00:38:48.380 --> 00:38:49.300
Oh, I didn't. Yeah.

00:38:49.440 --> 00:38:51.440
I mean like that's there. I just, I didn't think about that.

00:38:51.620 --> 00:38:52.800
So we're hitting him in his weak spot.

00:38:53.000 --> 00:38:54.640
Right. Yeah. That's cool.

00:38:55.280 --> 00:38:56.320
I like King Hippo.

00:38:57.060 --> 00:38:59.100
I like him too. I think he's a great fight character.

00:38:59.100 --> 00:39:04.640
I think this is the first one that actually like took some time to figure out the pattern.

00:39:05.300 --> 00:39:07.840
It's a little more difficult to solve the pattern.

00:39:07.980 --> 00:39:09.840
Yeah. The first time I faced him, I lost.

00:39:10.220 --> 00:39:12.880
Yeah. I think he's, I had no idea what to do.

00:39:13.040 --> 00:39:15.420
One of the most iconic people in this whole game.

00:39:15.580 --> 00:39:20.220
Yeah. And he shows up in all the games too, I think, including the Wii version that we'll talk about later.

00:39:21.180 --> 00:39:22.220
So that's King Hippo.

00:39:22.380 --> 00:39:26.580
He also shows up on the Captain N TV show, which we're not about to talk about today.

00:39:26.580 --> 00:39:28.720
Are you sure you don't want to go on a 15 minute?

00:39:29.460 --> 00:39:34.100
Yeah. And join back to our Donkey Kong days when we were talking about the Donkey Kong TV show,

00:39:34.260 --> 00:39:35.080
which was a thing.

00:39:35.240 --> 00:39:36.740
People, check it out on YouTube.

00:39:37.080 --> 00:39:38.960
It even got a Donkey Kong Junior show.

00:39:39.180 --> 00:39:40.340
Let's keep talking about Donkey Kong.

00:39:40.460 --> 00:39:42.820
Let's do this. Yeah. Why isn't Donkey Kong in this game?

00:39:43.200 --> 00:39:44.180
Wouldn't that be amazing?

00:39:44.300 --> 00:39:46.640
Isn't he? Isn't, oh no.

00:39:46.700 --> 00:39:48.360
You're thinking of in the Punch-Out!

00:39:48.400 --> 00:39:49.720
Arcade version, he's in the crowd.

00:39:49.720 --> 00:39:50.840
Oh, okay.

00:39:51.040 --> 00:39:54.920
Yeah, it's him, Mario, and somebody else.

00:39:55.320 --> 00:39:56.560
Probably Luigi. Cool.

00:39:56.560 --> 00:39:58.580
And then in Super Punch-Out!, I think even Yoshi's there.

00:39:58.900 --> 00:40:01.800
Aw. Which is weird that they let a dinosaur into the...

00:40:02.360 --> 00:40:03.980
He's an adorable dinosaur.

00:40:04.160 --> 00:40:08.420
Yes, he is. Speaking of adorable, we have Great Tiger up next.

00:40:08.760 --> 00:40:11.140
Not that adorable. Great Tiger is not that adorable.

00:40:11.140 --> 00:40:18.360
But he's pretty crazy because he has that onslaught of attacks that he does with his special,

00:40:18.360 --> 00:40:22.840
where if you thought Piston Honda was bad, he just keeps coming at you and at you.

00:40:22.920 --> 00:40:28.160
And this is the one for me where every time he came at me, I was very low on stamina

00:40:28.160 --> 00:40:32.200
and usually wound up, at the very least, if I wasn't knocked down by him,

00:40:32.360 --> 00:40:38.500
I was getting those pink flashes because I wasn't able to punch back because I was doing too much blocking.

00:40:38.740 --> 00:40:41.880
Mm-hmm. Right. This one took me a while to get through...

00:40:41.880 --> 00:40:44.360
Not a while, but it was difficult for me to get through the first round.

00:40:45.420 --> 00:40:48.400
But then in the second round, that's when he starts zipping around the ring.

00:40:48.500 --> 00:40:50.140
Yeah. And I was like, what's going on?

00:40:50.220 --> 00:40:51.260
He's teleporting.

00:40:51.420 --> 00:40:54.180
I was so panicked when all of a sudden he was just zooming around.

00:40:54.740 --> 00:40:56.560
That was the first thing that really...

00:40:57.060 --> 00:41:00.720
The first opponent that was really shockingly...

00:41:00.720 --> 00:41:04.300
But he's got the glowing head thing.

00:41:04.520 --> 00:41:06.180
On his turban, he has a jewel.

00:41:06.180 --> 00:41:07.200
Like a jewel.

00:41:07.540 --> 00:41:08.580
Yeah. And I like that.

00:41:08.640 --> 00:41:09.760
I thought that was neat. Mm-hmm.

00:41:10.060 --> 00:41:11.120
It's like, okay, sure.

00:41:11.960 --> 00:41:15.660
You know, like, it's better than the eyes glowing or the punch or something like that.

00:41:15.660 --> 00:41:16.660
So I'm down with it.

00:41:17.640 --> 00:41:20.820
Another thing I wanted to say about Great Tiger, though, besides the on-slave attacks,

00:41:20.980 --> 00:41:26.680
is that he does hint at the teleporting thing at the very beginning of the match when he gets out of the corner.

00:41:26.680 --> 00:41:29.040
He does teleport.

00:41:29.300 --> 00:41:33.940
Teleport. Ever so slightly. Yeah, like, teleport just a little bit further into the stage, which could look like a glitch.

00:41:34.300 --> 00:41:38.800
Oh, yeah. If you don't know the special move yet, it could just look like, oh, that was weird.

00:41:38.980 --> 00:41:41.000
Like, he just moved enough.

00:41:41.320 --> 00:41:44.680
Right. Like, it wasn't like he's teleporting around the entire ring.

00:41:45.000 --> 00:41:47.780
Yeah. It was like, get ready for this thing that's coming up.

00:41:48.080 --> 00:41:57.880
I actually really like that special move, though, despite how it requires, like, very precise timing of blocks or maybe a correct attack at the right time.

00:41:57.880 --> 00:42:01.840
I really thought that was interesting because now it's like we're in a different world.

00:42:02.000 --> 00:42:05.920
You know? Everybody so far has been grounded to reality in one way or another.

00:42:06.160 --> 00:42:10.080
Yeah. And then Great Tiger comes, and he's like, oh, by the way, I can teleport like Goku.

00:42:10.840 --> 00:42:15.740
Aside from maybe King Hippo not looking like a human being, but I get what you're saying.

00:42:15.740 --> 00:42:18.380
I'm sure somebody looks like that and they're offended by you saying that.

00:42:19.180 --> 00:42:21.300
I didn't notice it.

00:42:21.360 --> 00:42:26.400
I knew afterwards from watching Let's Plays and stuff, but I didn't notice it as teleporting.

00:42:26.460 --> 00:42:30.000
I thought it was just their way of showing him, like, running around, being really fast.

00:42:30.720 --> 00:42:32.340
But I guess this is supposed to be teleporting.

00:42:32.340 --> 00:42:33.240
Fast Joe. Yeah.

00:42:33.400 --> 00:42:34.580
That was a thing.

00:42:34.720 --> 00:42:37.220
He's just zipping around. Isn't that what Goku does?

00:42:37.380 --> 00:42:39.560
He just runs really fast and it looks like he's a teleporter?

00:42:39.760 --> 00:42:41.160
No, it's called instant transmission.

00:42:41.420 --> 00:42:43.040
Oh, I don't see. That shows what I know.

00:42:43.040 --> 00:42:45.680
He puts his two fingers on his forehead and it just happens.

00:42:45.700 --> 00:42:47.000
It goes, zink. Yeah.

00:42:47.420 --> 00:42:48.440
That's such a cool sound.

00:42:48.520 --> 00:42:51.400
Something like that. Yeah. And here's that sound right here.

00:42:51.760 --> 00:42:52.800
No, no, no.

00:42:53.700 --> 00:42:55.620
Yeah, this is now a Dragon Ball Z show.

00:42:57.060 --> 00:42:59.040
What do you guys think of Android 18?

00:42:59.040 --> 00:43:03.720
So, Bald Bull is the next character on our list.

00:43:03.880 --> 00:43:05.780
Does anybody want to talk about Bald Bull?

00:43:06.720 --> 00:43:07.940
No. Bald Bull.

00:43:10.400 --> 00:43:12.380
Bald Bull would be interesting, though.

00:43:13.220 --> 00:43:14.110
Bald Bull is...

00:43:15.740 --> 00:43:20.760
If there's one thing I'm going to say about this guy, it's that he's the equivalent to Piston

00:43:20.760 --> 00:43:23.340
Honda in terms of someone who actually looks like a boxer.

00:43:23.600 --> 00:43:24.920
Yes. Yeah, he's scary looking.

00:43:25.180 --> 00:43:29.280
Yeah. He definitely doesn't...

00:43:30.260 --> 00:43:35.520
And... I guess I don't know what I want to say here, but he's not as challenging to me as Great

00:43:35.520 --> 00:43:38.000
Tiger was, so it's weird that he comes afterwards.

00:43:38.400 --> 00:43:40.960
A bit of a respite to you, then?

00:43:41.080 --> 00:43:45.040
Like, oh, I'm recovering from this hard fight with a more easy fight?

00:43:45.140 --> 00:43:46.720
I don't think it's an easy fight, though, I guess.

00:43:46.720 --> 00:43:52.020
Because even though it's not as tricky as Great Tiger, I did find that Bald Bull's punches

00:43:52.020 --> 00:43:54.700
did an awful lot of damage to Little Mac.

00:43:54.900 --> 00:43:59.120
So, I actually found myself getting knocked down a lot, and that's tough because Bald Bull

00:43:59.120 --> 00:44:00.280
can't be KO'd.

00:44:00.640 --> 00:44:02.300
Yes. He can only...

00:44:02.300 --> 00:44:03.140
Can't be KO'd?

00:44:03.180 --> 00:44:04.780
He can only get TKO'd.

00:44:05.260 --> 00:44:06.360
Oh, oh. You can't...

00:44:06.360 --> 00:44:07.520
Knock him down three times, yeah.

00:44:07.600 --> 00:44:09.300
Exactly. You have to knock him down three times.

00:44:09.540 --> 00:44:14.020
He's unbeatable by decision, so if you go all three rounds, you won't be able to...

00:44:14.020 --> 00:44:18.660
They'll just decide that Bald Bull won the match, and I guess...

00:44:18.660 --> 00:44:20.240
Bald Bull. We'll just call him BB going forward.

00:44:20.300 --> 00:44:22.520
I think that's kind of BS, you know?

00:44:22.680 --> 00:44:26.820
Because if you're, like, a little kid, and you knock down this guy more than he knocks

00:44:26.820 --> 00:44:28.020
you down, I think you win.

00:44:28.460 --> 00:44:30.400
Yeah. I actually don't know.

00:44:30.540 --> 00:44:31.860
I've never won by decision.

00:44:32.060 --> 00:44:34.280
If I'm just going off a little bit here...

00:44:34.280 --> 00:44:37.420
Well, chances are, if it takes you three rounds to get through a fight, you're probably,

00:44:37.880 --> 00:44:39.480
you know, not doing that well.

00:44:39.480 --> 00:44:41.800
But is it possible? Yeah, but he's also not doing that well.

00:44:42.020 --> 00:44:43.960
You know? You know, he's also fighting me.

00:44:44.140 --> 00:44:45.240
Yeah, a 17-year-old boy.

00:44:45.240 --> 00:44:49.420
A 17-year-old, 107-pound boy, and he can't beat me, so what does that say about him?

00:44:49.560 --> 00:44:53.220
Yeah, if you go three rounds with Mike Tyson, I feel like you should just be decided the

00:44:53.220 --> 00:44:56.320
winner. Yeah. But, so, Joe, did you win a round?

00:44:56.320 --> 00:44:58.440
So, I did not win by decision, but I...

00:44:58.960 --> 00:45:03.020
Afterwards, after I stopped and couldn't get any farther, when I was watching a Let's

00:45:03.020 --> 00:45:07.080
Play, I saw, actually, against Mike Tyson, they won by decision.

00:45:07.220 --> 00:45:08.040
Really? Yeah. Okay.

00:45:08.300 --> 00:45:09.580
I thought it was... Oh, wow.

00:45:09.600 --> 00:45:11.080
I did not know that that was possible.

00:45:11.760 --> 00:45:13.380
So, with Bald Bull, I...

00:45:13.380 --> 00:45:19.200
Yeah. There's another one of those secret ways of ending him.

00:45:19.380 --> 00:45:24.100
Okay. Which is, when he does his little bull hop thing...

00:45:24.100 --> 00:45:25.680
Gotta hit him in the stomach again.

00:45:25.760 --> 00:45:27.760
Yeah, you hit him in the stomach on the third hop.

00:45:27.980 --> 00:45:31.580
He jumps towards you three times, and I think...

00:45:31.580 --> 00:45:32.640
And then he attacks you.

00:45:32.640 --> 00:45:36.260
And on the third hop, the second he jumps, or while he's...

00:45:36.260 --> 00:45:37.860
I'm not exactly sure what the timing is.

00:45:38.540 --> 00:45:41.080
If you do a body blow, then it just knocks him right out.

00:45:41.460 --> 00:45:42.920
Yeah. And I think you kind of, like...

00:45:42.920 --> 00:45:47.000
You have to do something like that, because he goes into, like, an infinite loop...

00:45:47.000 --> 00:45:48.360
Yeah. ...of that, you know?

00:45:48.440 --> 00:45:49.320
So, if you're...

00:45:49.320 --> 00:45:53.580
If you don't know what to do, you're just gonna have to continue blocking that infinite

00:45:53.580 --> 00:45:58.900
loop of him doing this weird leapfrog game, where he, like, backs up into the corner,

00:45:58.900 --> 00:46:00.240
and then boom, boom, boom.

00:46:00.640 --> 00:46:01.580
That's what I'm talking about.

00:46:01.800 --> 00:46:06.900
I think it's with 40 seconds left that always triggers, because I was trying over and over

00:46:06.900 --> 00:46:10.280
and over just to knock him down three times before that would happen.

00:46:10.500 --> 00:46:15.820
Yeah. But I think it also will jump into that if you knock him down twice, so that you can't just

00:46:15.820 --> 00:46:22.100
TKO... You can't TKO him before he does those charges.

00:46:22.360 --> 00:46:58.260
Yeah. And now we will move on to the World Circuit, where we see the repeat of Piston Honda.

00:46:59.220 --> 00:47:00.200
Um... Ball Ball.

00:47:02.300 --> 00:47:03.880
Uh... And Don Flamenco.

00:47:04.080 --> 00:47:05.780
They return. They're a little bit harder.

00:47:06.040 --> 00:47:09.420
I don't want to talk about them again, though, but one thing I did want to call out that

00:47:09.420 --> 00:47:15.340
I watched a YouTube video on was that the second time you fight Piston Honda, there is...

00:47:15.340 --> 00:47:20.320
And it was known in the community for a long time that there's a special timing for a punch

00:47:20.320 --> 00:47:23.620
that will knock out Piston Honda and end the match.

00:47:23.620 --> 00:47:29.560
Wow. But nobody could figure out how to, like, what was the timing or when to do it, but there

00:47:29.560 --> 00:47:35.740
actually is a visual cue in the game that was staring at gamers all across the globe

00:47:35.740 --> 00:47:40.300
for 20 years before it was finally discovered about a year or two ago.

00:47:40.300 --> 00:47:45.580
So, in the audience, those pink characters with the black silhouettes, you know, uh, who I'm

00:47:45.580 --> 00:47:46.860
talking about, like, all the little heads.

00:47:47.100 --> 00:47:52.760
Mm-hmm. In the left side, right behind, like, right behind where Piston Honda would be standing,

00:47:53.380 --> 00:47:57.760
as soon as the timing comes, the audience member ducks.

00:47:58.580 --> 00:48:00.040
They actually duck.

00:48:00.040 --> 00:48:05.020
And if you punch, if you give him an uppercut, as soon as that character ducks, which only

00:48:05.020 --> 00:48:09.320
happens in that round, you will knock him out and it will be a TKO.

00:48:09.320 --> 00:48:10.180
That's actually amazing.

00:48:10.360 --> 00:48:15.140
I want a spinoff game about that character and him trying to give you hints.

00:48:15.680 --> 00:48:16.680
Right? Like, what does he know?

00:48:16.820 --> 00:48:18.600
Isn't there a camera one also?

00:48:18.880 --> 00:48:25.540
I think there's a few video audience cues, but that one stood out the most interesting

00:48:25.540 --> 00:48:27.960
to me because it wasn't discovered for 20 years.

00:48:27.960 --> 00:48:33.080
There had to have been some kid in 1987 or a few of them that knew about it, like, found

00:48:33.080 --> 00:48:35.060
out and just kept their mouth shut for so long.

00:48:35.820 --> 00:48:37.240
Yeah, I'm keeping this to myself.

00:48:38.260 --> 00:48:39.440
I just think it's really cool, though.

00:48:39.600 --> 00:48:40.840
I mean, I never looked at the audience.

00:48:41.200 --> 00:48:45.400
I know there was some guy taking a picture, but I didn't think, oh, I should punch the guy

00:48:45.400 --> 00:48:46.360
while he's taking the picture.

00:48:47.420 --> 00:48:51.300
But we do face one new character, and that's Soda Popinski.

00:48:52.360 --> 00:48:56.020
Very Russian. Originally Vodka Drunkinski.

00:48:56.320 --> 00:49:00.400
More Russian. Yeah, that was censored by Nintendo of America.

00:49:00.820 --> 00:49:04.540
There were some unfortunate stereotypes going on at some point.

00:49:04.720 --> 00:49:09.360
And, yeah, he's very Russian, to Sean's point, so that's probably why they didn't want to

00:49:09.360 --> 00:49:11.360
do the Vodka Drunkinski.

00:49:12.100 --> 00:49:14.340
So, anything to say about Soda?

00:49:15.460 --> 00:49:18.160
I honestly don't think I made it to him.

00:49:18.180 --> 00:49:21.920
I think I could only get through the repeats.

00:49:21.920 --> 00:49:23.780
Okay. Well, you don't fade it.

00:49:23.860 --> 00:49:25.100
I don't think the repeats, sorry.

00:49:25.200 --> 00:49:30.560
I don't think it's Piston Honda, Ball, Ball, and Don Flamenco before Soda, is it?

00:49:30.820 --> 00:49:33.760
I only played a couple guys I had already played before.

00:49:33.960 --> 00:49:40.740
I'm pretty sure, yeah. Anyway, Soda Popinski, the long story short on him is that it's just at this point in the

00:49:40.740 --> 00:49:43.900
game, all of the fights are very challenging, in my opinion.

00:49:44.420 --> 00:49:49.160
This is the kind of, once you get to the world circuit, we're at the point now where it's

00:49:49.160 --> 00:49:52.180
going to come down to a few times playing through each character.

00:49:52.560 --> 00:49:56.320
Just to get, like, the groove of how to fight them, what to dodge.

00:49:56.320 --> 00:50:01.200
And even when you get up to them, there's no guarantee that you're going to perform perfectly.

00:50:01.520 --> 00:50:06.040
Yeah. You know, it's sad to say, but I feel like the next three characters, Mr.

00:50:06.140 --> 00:50:11.840
Sandman, Super Macho Man, and Mike Tyson, for the average player who's not obsessed with Mike Tyson's

00:50:11.840 --> 00:50:15.080
punch out, they really don't stand a chance against those three guys.

00:50:15.380 --> 00:50:17.880
Yeah. Well, I'm living proof of that.

00:50:18.340 --> 00:50:22.440
Definitely. Not definitely you, but also definitely you.

00:50:23.260 --> 00:50:27.980
Mr. Sandman was as far as I got to anything.

00:50:28.160 --> 00:50:29.160
Same with me. Yeah.

00:50:29.380 --> 00:50:32.940
And I did not last more than the first round with Mr.

00:50:33.020 --> 00:50:38.200
Sandman. Even just trying to dodge things, my timing was just very awful.

00:50:38.740 --> 00:50:43.500
I was constantly getting uppercuts hit on me, and he takes off a massive amount.

00:50:43.540 --> 00:50:45.600
He hit on you, too? Yeah.

00:50:47.060 --> 00:50:50.540
Mr. Sandman not only brought me a dream, but he hit on me.

00:50:51.760 --> 00:50:54.540
Jeez. They really want to just embarrass you here.

00:50:54.600 --> 00:50:59.920
So I don't want to say that the game isn't fair, because it is definitely fair.

00:51:00.080 --> 00:51:04.820
It requires a certain dexterity that I do not have.

00:51:04.900 --> 00:51:12.280
I do not have the capacity to time my dodges and my punches to match these guys.

00:51:12.420 --> 00:51:14.080
These guys are playing on...

00:51:14.080 --> 00:51:15.620
Four-dimensional chess here.

00:51:15.620 --> 00:51:17.780
Yeah, four-dimensional chess, but also steroids.

00:51:17.780 --> 00:51:22.100
I think it also just requires an amount of practice.

00:51:22.500 --> 00:51:25.040
Yeah. That's what I think is so cool about these games is that it's...

00:51:25.040 --> 00:51:28.040
I think it's the first time we've seen boss battles like this.

00:51:28.220 --> 00:51:33.440
I mean, that are... That you really have to learn and practice and get that satisfaction of beating a boss afterwards.

00:51:33.600 --> 00:51:37.760
I mean, I think the closest thing was maybe Twin B had some things we had to learn what was happening.

00:51:37.760 --> 00:51:39.300
This is the first boss rush game, too.

00:51:39.520 --> 00:51:42.140
But this is the first real boss that you have to...

00:51:42.140 --> 00:51:44.780
I mean, every battle, every fight, every battle.

00:51:45.120 --> 00:51:45.960
I'm not a boxer.

00:51:46.360 --> 00:51:48.300
You were talking about Stinger there, right?

00:51:49.680 --> 00:51:50.840
Twin B, we didn't...

00:51:50.840 --> 00:51:53.080
Twin B is the Japanese version?

00:51:53.340 --> 00:51:54.620
It's the Japanese version. Okay, yes. Stinger.

00:51:56.500 --> 00:51:59.780
Stinger, yep.

00:51:59.780 --> 00:52:05.340
You described this to me, though, earlier this week, that this game is essentially a boss rush.

00:52:05.460 --> 00:52:12.940
Right. And I love that you said that, because I think that's the closest thing we've ever had to a boss rush in any NES game so far.

00:52:13.340 --> 00:52:14.560
I just had the realization.

00:52:14.820 --> 00:52:20.400
Right. Imagine if you had to play, like, Legend of Zelda on Dungeon 8 right before Ganon.

00:52:20.480 --> 00:52:24.740
You have to fight every other villain again before you can get to Ganon.

00:52:24.860 --> 00:52:25.820
Like, that would be ridiculous.

00:52:26.360 --> 00:52:28.000
Here, that's the whole game.

00:52:28.240 --> 00:52:29.980
Right. Wow.

00:52:30.760 --> 00:52:34.540
And it feels to me a little more like modern boss battles.

00:52:35.080 --> 00:52:36.860
Just because of the way...

00:52:36.860 --> 00:52:39.440
On the NES, it's usually a little thing.

00:52:39.540 --> 00:52:41.880
Oh, you gotta hit it in that weak spot, and then you gotta figure that out.

00:52:41.920 --> 00:52:43.020
Or when this opens up, you hit it.

00:52:43.080 --> 00:52:47.520
But this is really pattern memory, or pattern recognition.

00:52:47.720 --> 00:52:52.360
When we were talking about the tells earlier, I wasn't sure if I was going to be ridiculed for this,

00:52:52.480 --> 00:52:57.580
but I was looking at Joe, and Joe is a big Shadow of the Colossus fan and played through that entire game.

00:52:57.580 --> 00:53:08.300
And now that we're talking about it, I need to ask, are there any similarities for you here between the Colossi in Shadow of the Colossus and how you deal with them,

00:53:08.320 --> 00:53:12.640
and then, like, these 8-bit interpretations of bosses here?

00:53:12.640 --> 00:53:15.860
I mean, now that you mention it, I hadn't thought of it that way, but now that you mention it, yeah.

00:53:16.200 --> 00:53:23.940
I mean, obviously, if you take away the obvious differences of Shadow of the Colossus being this giant, beautiful 3D rendered thing,

00:53:24.080 --> 00:53:29.300
but it's just the idea of recognizing, okay, what is this guy's quirk, and what do you do?

00:53:29.300 --> 00:53:34.540
I actually thought a little bit of it, this is also, I don't want to be ridiculed for this either,

00:53:34.740 --> 00:53:40.440
but I was feeling the same feeling that I get when I'm fighting a boss in Kingdom Hearts.

00:53:41.140 --> 00:53:45.020
Because obviously those bosses are very different, you're running around, there's a lot more strategy,

00:53:45.220 --> 00:53:50.120
but it's still, you're seeing, okay, so once I do this, he's going to start doing this move now,

00:53:50.240 --> 00:53:51.720
and I've got to think of how to counter that.

00:53:51.720 --> 00:54:01.200
I think any well-designed, like, 3D character action game that has the concept of bosses has this to some extent.

00:54:01.400 --> 00:54:03.880
Right, definitely, and I think that's why this is amazing to me.

00:54:03.980 --> 00:54:10.060
It's the first one that feels like what most 3D boss battles feel like, and it's just not in the NES.

00:54:10.220 --> 00:54:17.140
You're not moving. I haven't played it, but this game reminded me of kind of what people talk about with Dark Souls,

00:54:17.740 --> 00:54:20.000
you know, with dodging and countering and everything.

00:54:20.240 --> 00:54:23.380
Okay. And I don't know if that's accurate because I haven't played it.

00:54:23.660 --> 00:54:30.640
I think that, again, I think that Dark Souls, while it's more difficult than a lot of games,

00:54:30.820 --> 00:54:36.440
it still borrows heavily from every sort of action game that came before it,

00:54:36.580 --> 00:54:45.060
at least in how it designs bosses, except that every different enemy in that game is sort of designed like a boss

00:54:45.840 --> 00:54:48.620
with tells and when you should attack and when you shouldn't.

00:54:48.620 --> 00:54:50.240
I like that we're having this discussion.

00:54:50.600 --> 00:54:56.980
I think it goes back to what Sean said, how every good boss fight should be feeling this way.

00:54:57.720 --> 00:55:00.560
And so I don't mean to say that when you get to Mr.

00:55:00.720 --> 00:55:02.560
Sandman, Super Macho Man and Mike Tyson,

00:55:02.780 --> 00:55:04.240
that all of a sudden it's a bad game.

00:55:04.360 --> 00:55:11.700
But it is noticeably different that the other ones or the other fights all up until then were more approachable and maybe took some time.

00:55:11.760 --> 00:55:14.320
And I'm sure if I had more time, maybe I would learn Mr.

00:55:14.440 --> 00:55:17.460
Sandman. And then slowly more time I'd get through Super Macho Man.

00:55:17.540 --> 00:55:19.280
And who knows, maybe one day I'd beat Mike Tyson.

00:55:19.280 --> 00:55:25.860
I know that a lot of people all over the globe would talk about the NES and Mike Tyson's Punch-Out specifically

00:55:25.860 --> 00:55:27.940
because it is considered one of the hardest NES games.

00:55:28.180 --> 00:55:33.020
There are a lot of people who also consider it a very fair game, similar to how we talk about Ghosts and Goblins,

00:55:33.060 --> 00:55:34.900
which not many people consider a fair game.

00:55:34.900 --> 00:55:38.000
But I never felt like it was punishing us in a way that we didn't deserve,

00:55:38.020 --> 00:55:42.980
other than maybe the Red Devil onslaught, which was maybe our first example of a boss rush,

00:55:43.740 --> 00:55:46.000
even though it's just a minor character in the game.

00:55:46.000 --> 00:55:50.300
But all that said, I kind of wish that Mr.

00:55:50.380 --> 00:55:51.780
Sandman, Super Macho Man, and Mike Tyson

00:55:51.780 --> 00:55:58.700
only ramped up slightly each time in difficulty instead of all of a sudden this floor that I couldn't overcome.

00:55:59.080 --> 00:56:03.760
Yeah, I think that difficulty curves are still a thing that everybody is working on right now,

00:56:03.880 --> 00:56:10.680
not only just for, again, prolonging the amount of time that it takes to finish a game.

00:56:10.900 --> 00:56:12.620
You're spending 60 bucks on the game.

00:56:12.720 --> 00:56:15.640
Yeah, but also just for game design in general.

00:56:16.000 --> 00:56:19.760
I do think, though, that because of the way the save system works,

00:56:20.420 --> 00:56:25.660
I mean, not that anyone said it wasn't fair, but I do think it's very fair in that it doesn't put you all the way back at the beginning.

00:56:25.800 --> 00:56:28.180
Yeah. You know, it sends you, what, to the beginning of the world circuit?

00:56:28.360 --> 00:56:29.260
The beginning of the circuit that you're on, yeah.

00:56:29.260 --> 00:56:31.740
Which is still a lot of people to get back to Mr.

00:56:31.860 --> 00:56:35.100
Sandman, but I think it's still...

00:56:35.100 --> 00:56:38.340
It's more fair than, say, going to have to start with Glass Joe again.

00:56:38.420 --> 00:56:39.300
Absolutely. Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:56:39.520 --> 00:56:42.780
So I think that's kind of, it's kind of cool because it doesn't set you back that much.

00:56:42.780 --> 00:56:48.920
And then by the end of it, you can breeze through, you know, Pissin' Honda, Bald Bull, and...

00:56:48.920 --> 00:56:54.060
Bald Bull. The rural juror.

00:56:55.380 --> 00:56:56.740
And Donnie Flamingo.

00:56:56.860 --> 00:56:58.180
Yeah, Donnie Flamingo.

00:56:58.240 --> 00:56:59.260
I like that. Yeah.

00:56:59.260 --> 00:57:03.920
So I would have liked the inclusion of a practice mode, maybe, where you could pick who you want

00:57:03.920 --> 00:57:07.560
to fight against just to learn the ropes, and it wouldn't count for anything, but you could

00:57:07.560 --> 00:57:09.020
learn how to play against the characters.

00:57:09.260 --> 00:57:13.240
But I could see how that also goes against the spirit of the game and getting the title.

00:57:13.420 --> 00:57:17.740
It's like, hey, Bald Bull, do you want to have a practice match in the back?

00:57:17.920 --> 00:57:19.240
Like, that wouldn't happen.

00:57:19.420 --> 00:57:20.360
Yeah. I don't think so.

00:57:20.540 --> 00:57:21.480
It goes against the lore.

00:57:21.600 --> 00:57:23.260
There is something interesting to point out.

00:57:23.800 --> 00:57:30.520
We're now on a whole different level of talking about video games right now, but in SNK's 40th

00:57:30.520 --> 00:57:37.260
anniversary compilation, you can actually watch that game play itself, and then at any moment

00:57:37.260 --> 00:57:38.860
say, I'd like to take control.

00:57:39.060 --> 00:57:44.920
And so you can just watch the whole game play itself, get to the final battle, and say, I'm

00:57:44.920 --> 00:57:47.500
ready to jump in and do that.

00:57:47.640 --> 00:57:51.620
And, you know, that kind of accessibility in this day and age is nice.

00:57:51.620 --> 00:57:57.320
I'm not saying it would have worked in 1987 to just watch a computer little Mac fight

00:57:57.320 --> 00:57:59.520
everybody and then be like, oh, cool, he's back up to Mr.

00:57:59.620 --> 00:58:00.780
Sandman. It's my turn now.

00:58:00.800 --> 00:58:08.020
I feel like stuff like that only happens when a game becomes so incredibly niche or has evolved

00:58:08.940 --> 00:58:11.760
inside of itself so much that fighting games have become.

00:58:11.920 --> 00:58:13.100
Think about what we're talking about, too.

00:58:13.180 --> 00:58:15.260
We're talking about a 40th anniversary compilation.

00:58:15.540 --> 00:58:17.660
Like, of course, it would have a feature like that.

00:58:17.740 --> 00:58:19.080
You know, it's a museum thing.

00:58:19.540 --> 00:58:21.640
It's not like, yeah, you know what I'm saying?

00:58:21.640 --> 00:58:25.380
It's not like you're about to go play, you know, Kingdom Hearts 3.

00:58:26.060 --> 00:58:27.140
He's going to let it play.

00:58:27.880 --> 00:58:29.240
Yeah, and just let it play.

00:58:29.680 --> 00:58:31.580
Like a whole, like, adventure game.

00:58:31.920 --> 00:58:35.160
Right. They're never going to put that in there because the whole point of the game was for you to

00:58:35.160 --> 00:58:38.780
play it. Yeah. So I'm just throwing that in hindsight.

00:58:38.800 --> 00:58:42.340
