01:09:54.000 --> 01:09:56.140
All right. Sequels and spinoffs time.

01:09:56.420 --> 01:09:58.780
Here we go. Super Punch-Out!

01:09:59.640 --> 01:10:02.800
Comes out in arcades before this game.

01:10:04.280 --> 01:10:06.320
Oh. So Mike Tyson's Punch-Out!

01:10:06.360 --> 01:10:10.060
is released in 1987 on the NES, but Super Punch-Out!

01:10:10.180 --> 01:10:12.240
comes out in the arcade in 1985.

01:10:12.900 --> 01:10:14.560
So this is like a demake?

01:10:15.360 --> 01:10:21.000
Right? In some ways, it's like we cannot possibly port the Super Punch-Out!

01:10:21.060 --> 01:10:24.840
version of this game to the NES, so we'll just give them the regular Punch-Out!

01:10:24.840 --> 01:10:26.980
So, hold on.

01:10:27.560 --> 01:10:28.800
The order goes Punch-Out!

01:10:28.820 --> 01:10:31.100
for arcade in 84, Super Punch-Out!

01:10:31.160 --> 01:10:33.260
in 85, Mike Tyson's Punch-Out!

01:10:33.260 --> 01:10:37.080
in 87, um, regular Punch-Out!

01:10:37.180 --> 01:10:39.520
on NES, where it replaces Mike Tyson with Mr.

01:10:39.680 --> 01:10:43.840
Dream in 90, then Super Punch-Out!

01:10:43.920 --> 01:10:49.100
for Super Nintendo in 1994, and then Punch-Out!

01:10:49.180 --> 01:10:54.380
goes like dark for a really long time before it returns in 2009.

01:10:55.040 --> 01:10:55.900
for the Wii.

01:10:56.640 --> 01:11:00.440
Yes. And, uh, they took on a really cool style for that game.

01:11:00.600 --> 01:11:04.140
I don't want to say cel-shaded, but definitely like cartoon-looking.

01:11:04.420 --> 01:11:10.160
Yeah. And you use the Wiimote and the Nunchuck as your, um, one-two punch kind of thing.

01:11:10.420 --> 01:11:16.720
And then you can even use the balance board to, uh, to sway to the left and right to dodge.

01:11:16.860 --> 01:11:21.400
I think that that's a pretty unique stance for the, I mean, like how many other games is the balance board?

01:11:21.540 --> 01:11:22.520
It's basically VR. VR. Yeah.

01:11:23.100 --> 01:11:25.560
I've, I always felt like every Wii game was VR.

01:11:27.400 --> 01:11:30.380
Uh, and, and then if that wasn't weird enough, Punch-Out!

01:11:30.400 --> 01:11:33.060
making its like random return in 2009 for the Wii,

01:11:33.820 --> 01:11:35.340
Doc Lewis's Punch-Out!

01:11:35.700 --> 01:11:37.760
gets released for WiiWare.

01:11:37.960 --> 01:11:40.480
Is this, do you take the role as a corner man?

01:11:40.480 --> 01:11:44.580
It's just a, it's, no, you, you, you just, it's a training game.

01:11:44.780 --> 01:11:46.040
It's just, it's like a training mode.

01:11:46.400 --> 01:11:49.580
Oh, I see. It's a standalone prequel to Punch-Out!

01:11:49.860 --> 01:11:53.320
where you're essentially just training for the minor circuit.

01:11:53.660 --> 01:11:55.040
Huh. Wow.

01:11:55.300 --> 01:11:57.260
Yep. So wait, what do you do in that game?

01:11:57.420 --> 01:12:03.200
You're just, uh, you're just doing like practice essentially with, it's, it's just, so for those that don't know,

01:12:03.200 --> 01:12:11.180
so WiiWare was like what they called these downloadable, like very cheap games that you could only get on the Wii Shop.

01:12:11.580 --> 01:12:16.340
Okay. For like, and it's only for a limited time too, so you couldn't actually get Doc Lewis's Punch-Out!

01:12:16.440 --> 01:12:20.060
anymore. There's only, there's no way to play that game other than via Dolphin, I guess.

01:12:20.400 --> 01:12:26.420
Interesting. And so in that game you were essentially just doing like target practice and maybe like a fight or two.

01:12:26.920 --> 01:12:29.480
It was not a full game with a story mode and everything.

01:12:29.780 --> 01:12:36.720
Wow. Yep. Then Little Mac, of course, joins the fight in Super Smash Brothers for Wii U and 3DS.

01:12:37.240 --> 01:12:41.100
And Sam, as our resident Smash Brothers guy, how do you feel about Little Mac in Smash Brothers?

01:12:41.320 --> 01:12:45.740
I personally don't like fighting him, but, you know, he's just a new character, so you gotta get used to it.

01:12:45.920 --> 01:12:48.640
And I think that it's, I think that he's, it's a really cool addition.

01:12:48.640 --> 01:12:52.560
I think that he suffers a lot in Smash Ultimate because he has no recovery.

01:12:53.060 --> 01:12:55.280
Yeah. Is he, is he new to Ultimate?

01:12:55.600 --> 01:12:57.480
No, he's new to Smash 4.

01:12:57.680 --> 01:13:02.180
Yeah. I don't like that he has the, I forget what it's called, the, um.

01:13:02.500 --> 01:13:05.580
The KO? The KO punch, the, the uppercut thing.

01:13:05.800 --> 01:13:07.240
That you just press B and someone dies.

01:13:07.360 --> 01:13:08.800
I don't like that, but that's just me.

01:13:09.340 --> 01:13:13.820
It was a real, so that is a little confusing though, but that was in the Punch-Out!

01:13:13.860 --> 01:13:15.260
Arcade game. Okay.

01:13:15.260 --> 01:13:19.200
There is a meter that builds up as you play well in Punch-Out!

01:13:19.300 --> 01:13:22.540
Yeah. I'm just not crazy about that as a mechanic in Smash Bros.

01:13:22.740 --> 01:13:26.720
How faithful do you feel like he translates over though from playing Punch-Out!

01:13:26.800 --> 01:13:31.580
to this? I feel like, I feel like now I have to go back and play as Little Mac in both of these games

01:13:31.580 --> 01:13:32.440
to really see.

01:13:32.600 --> 01:13:34.400
Because I think that he is really.

01:13:34.400 --> 01:13:36.220
I think it's more like a spirit of Little Mac.

01:13:36.320 --> 01:13:40.300
Yeah. It doesn't really like translate how you play as Little Mac in, in Punch-Out!

01:13:40.300 --> 01:13:45.060
I'm thinking about the moves because his side B is like the, it's like the star punch,

01:13:45.520 --> 01:13:46.880
you know, where he lunges.

01:13:47.120 --> 01:13:53.240
Yeah. The aerial moves are kind of whatever because it's not really anything.

01:13:53.780 --> 01:13:57.420
Yeah. It's mostly just punches though, which is nice.

01:13:57.600 --> 01:14:00.900
He's little and he's fast, which I guess is what, you know, what it is.

01:14:00.900 --> 01:14:03.920
I just think it's nice they didn't give him anything like stupid, you know?

01:14:04.160 --> 01:14:05.320
They didn't give him a sword or a gun.

01:14:05.320 --> 01:14:11.260
Right, exactly. Or like any accessories, like, imagine if he just like, well imagine if he just like

01:14:11.260 --> 01:14:14.620
threw like one of those boxing bag thing, you know, like punching bags.

01:14:14.640 --> 01:14:16.960
Like if he brought one of those out, like that's dumb.

01:14:17.100 --> 01:14:20.100
It would have been more like the design of Duck Hunt Dog.

01:14:21.560 --> 01:14:25.180
Yeah, he's like working with Mike Tyson, he's like lifting him up and stuff.

01:14:26.200 --> 01:14:27.140
Yeah, it's funny.

01:14:27.660 --> 01:14:28.520
I like that.

01:14:28.860 --> 01:14:35.360
This is why we talk. I think as far as, you know, a Smash Brothers version of him, I think they did a pretty good

01:14:35.360 --> 01:14:36.300
job. Cool.

01:14:36.500 --> 01:14:40.300
See, for me as someone who doesn't, I'm not a Smash Brothers guy, I'm pretty bad at the

01:14:40.300 --> 01:14:45.100
game, but I do, when I do play, I always kind of play around with different characters and

01:14:45.100 --> 01:14:48.560
see once in a while, I'll do okay with a character and I'll be like, oh, that's going to be my

01:14:48.560 --> 01:14:49.400
character from now on.

01:14:49.500 --> 01:14:50.480
It's really just a fluke.

01:14:52.080 --> 01:14:56.920
But Little Mac was one of those in Smash Brothers Ultimate and I was doing really good with them.

01:14:56.920 --> 01:15:01.580
Not, but I do agree with you about the one hit KO punch, kind of cheap.

01:15:02.120 --> 01:15:07.260
But I, without really remembering too much of how he played, because I'm not a big Smash

01:15:07.260 --> 01:15:09.800
Brothers guy in general, I really liked playing as him.

01:15:10.160 --> 01:15:16.300
Yeah. I think he's fun and I think he's good for beginners to get like a good upper hand on

01:15:16.300 --> 01:15:17.880
people because he does a ton of damage.

01:15:18.320 --> 01:15:23.620
Yeah. So as long as you're willing to go out there and connect some punches, you'll punch out

01:15:23.620 --> 01:15:24.700
your opponent. Ha!

01:15:25.260 --> 01:15:26.260
Name drop. We did it.

01:15:26.260 --> 01:15:31.980
Yep. It is time now for a little game we like to call The Essential Games List.

01:15:34.940 --> 01:15:40.680
Guys, today we are voting on Mike Tyson's Punch Out for the unaware listener who just listened

01:15:40.680 --> 01:15:45.740
to about an hour of us talking about it and is now like, oh, they're voting on Mike Tyson's

01:15:45.740 --> 01:15:47.240
Punch Out? Not Smash.

01:15:47.420 --> 01:15:48.240
Not Smash, yeah.

01:15:48.440 --> 01:15:50.100
We will get there some year.

01:15:50.360 --> 01:15:52.180
Yeah. 3,060 or something.

01:15:52.180 --> 01:15:54.880
But right now we're just talking about Mike Tyson's Punch Out.

01:15:55.040 --> 01:15:57.620
We're voting whether it is essential today.

01:15:58.040 --> 01:16:01.820
Not talking about 1987, not talking about that 1984 arcade port.

01:16:02.060 --> 01:16:05.220
I'm talking about, is this game still worth playing today?

01:16:05.500 --> 01:16:06.460
Does it hold up?

01:16:07.060 --> 01:16:11.500
Sam. I think this game is essential.

01:16:11.540 --> 01:16:13.080
I'm going to say it right off the bat.

01:16:13.340 --> 01:16:14.720
I think that there's... Go all in, baby.

01:16:14.840 --> 01:16:15.700
There's no...

01:16:15.700 --> 01:16:20.360
I think this is one of the most essential games for the NES that we've played.

01:16:20.360 --> 01:16:25.400
I think that playing this game, there's really no other game like it.

01:16:25.720 --> 01:16:26.980
It has all the...

01:16:26.980 --> 01:16:30.960
Like, the 8-bit graphics are charming.

01:16:31.280 --> 01:16:33.160
You know, it doesn't look weird or anything.

01:16:33.380 --> 01:16:38.320
Everyone has like a weird personality to their characters.

01:16:38.320 --> 01:16:43.280
There's... The mechanics are simple, but they're really, you know, easy to figure out.

01:16:43.360 --> 01:16:46.360
But it's complicated to beat all the characters.

01:16:46.780 --> 01:16:47.880
You know, it's got music.

01:16:47.880 --> 01:16:53.800
It's got that killer cut scene when you're running and possibly what is Staten Island, maybe Battery Park.

01:16:53.920 --> 01:16:55.740
We're not really sure. And...

01:16:55.740 --> 01:16:57.020
This is very important.

01:16:58.920 --> 01:17:00.120
And it's hard.

01:17:00.420 --> 01:17:02.920
It's that kind of Nintendo hard that you hear about.

01:17:03.100 --> 01:17:04.480
But I think that it's a...

01:17:04.480 --> 01:17:06.140
Look, we talked about it. I think it's a fair hard.

01:17:06.300 --> 01:17:09.520
I think it is an essential NES game.

01:17:09.680 --> 01:17:11.520
And just an essential game in general.

01:17:12.060 --> 01:17:13.180
Great. Joe?

01:17:13.400 --> 01:17:19.300
So I came into this with these two conflicting notions about this game.

01:17:19.300 --> 01:17:24.640
One was my, so far, previous experience with games that are trying to simulate some sort of fighting.

01:17:24.780 --> 01:17:28.780
Boxing or wrestling. Which have been very bad so far up until this point.

01:17:29.200 --> 01:17:34.400
But the other was knowing that this is a really popular, famous game that everyone's nostalgic for.

01:17:34.640 --> 01:17:37.840
So, like I said in the beginning, I kind of thought that this was going to be like...

01:17:37.840 --> 01:17:41.360
One of those games that people misremember as being good.

01:17:41.380 --> 01:17:42.360
And it's really not that great.

01:17:42.520 --> 01:17:45.200
But I was pleasantly surprised to find...

01:17:45.200 --> 01:17:48.800
This is, I think, one of the best games we've played so far.

01:17:48.800 --> 01:17:49.660
Gameplay wise.

01:17:50.120 --> 01:17:54.280
And I think, especially because of the way that you learn a boss.

01:17:54.460 --> 01:18:00.080
I think that what we were talking about the boss rush element of it is what really makes it stand out to me.

01:18:00.200 --> 01:18:04.620
That it feels like a satisfying experience for each battle.

01:18:04.640 --> 01:18:06.920
Especially the more difficult ones that come later on.

01:18:07.040 --> 01:18:09.060
To have struggled with it for a while.

01:18:09.240 --> 01:18:10.060
Slowly learn it.

01:18:10.160 --> 01:18:10.980
Understand the patterns.

01:18:11.220 --> 01:18:14.220
And that moment when you finally beat it is just so...

01:18:14.220 --> 01:18:15.920
There's something so satisfying about it.

01:18:15.940 --> 01:18:17.960
Similar to the way games are now.

01:18:18.800 --> 01:18:20.140
Sorry, I'm getting a little long-winded here.

01:18:20.180 --> 01:18:21.880
No, it's okay. It's very beautiful.

01:18:22.340 --> 01:18:26.360
And Sam mentioned the Nintendo hard idea.

01:18:26.560 --> 01:18:31.980
And I think this is that Nintendo hard, but not the negative side.

01:18:32.040 --> 01:18:33.060
Not the jank hard.

01:18:33.060 --> 01:18:35.260
Yeah, not the negative side of Nintendo hard where it's just...

01:18:35.260 --> 01:18:40.860
I mean, I'm kind of just mirroring what you just said, but where it's just bad game design.

01:18:40.860 --> 01:18:45.940
It's hard where it's a challenge that you can learn and you can actually get better at it.

01:18:46.600 --> 01:18:48.040
So, no, not a...

01:18:48.040 --> 01:18:49.000
No, I'm just kidding. Yes.

01:18:49.840 --> 01:18:51.620
Definitely essential. Okay.

01:18:51.980 --> 01:18:56.020
If Sean votes it in, then it will be an essential game.

01:18:56.240 --> 01:18:57.940
Sean? All right.

01:18:58.100 --> 01:19:01.640
So, I come into this again struggling...

01:19:02.620 --> 01:19:08.120
Not struggling, but wrestling with the idea of what the essential games list means.

01:19:09.400 --> 01:19:16.060
And when it comes to punch out, we already know that there is a super punch out.

01:19:16.560 --> 01:19:19.840
I haven't really played too much of super punch out.

01:19:19.980 --> 01:19:24.640
So, I don't know if it is just a flat out better version of this game.

01:19:24.820 --> 01:19:33.380
So, I have to think about Mike Tyson's punch out as the only punch out that I've ever played.

01:19:34.780 --> 01:19:44.480
But regardless of whether one's better than the other, I think that the series as a whole itself is just a unique enough experience.

01:19:45.340 --> 01:19:52.820
But regardless of whether one game is better than the other within the same series, since I've only played one,

01:19:52.960 --> 01:19:59.820
and I agree with everything that Sam and Joe has said about it in regards to how good this game is, so I won't reiterate,

01:19:59.820 --> 01:20:04.940
I think that this game is also essential, even if super punch out may be better.

01:20:05.120 --> 01:20:08.660
I haven't played it. There's a lot of things that make a game non-essential.

01:20:08.980 --> 01:20:14.420
I think I check off more boxes right off the bat when I start a game that make it not essential

01:20:14.420 --> 01:20:17.260
than would ever make me consider it being essential.

01:20:17.540 --> 01:20:20.800
I actually, when I judge these games, I actually think,

01:20:20.960 --> 01:20:24.480
why wouldn't this game be on the essential games list rather than why it should be on?

01:20:25.100 --> 01:20:31.380
Then there's certain games that are Nintendo-made games that are very well-known games in the history of video games.

01:20:31.520 --> 01:20:37.460
We've dealt with this a few times now with Super Mario Brothers, Legend of Zelda, Metroid, and now Mike Tyson's punch out.

01:20:37.560 --> 01:20:43.540
These are the four games that I think a lot of people just know that they're essential games,

01:20:43.560 --> 01:20:46.480
and it's up to us to kind of put our own spin on that,

01:20:46.580 --> 01:20:50.840
even if you suspected that we were going to vote Super Mario Brothers in as essential,

01:20:50.860 --> 01:20:52.640
or The Legend of Zelda in as essential.

01:20:53.940 --> 01:20:57.540
Sometimes you have played those games before, sometimes you haven't.

01:20:57.760 --> 01:21:00.480
I didn't play Mike Tyson's punch out before we started doing this.

01:21:00.620 --> 01:21:03.900
I honestly didn't even see too much of that game beforehand.

01:21:03.900 --> 01:21:06.600
I didn't know how to play.

01:21:06.600 --> 01:21:08.820
I didn't see any videos that often.

01:21:09.120 --> 01:21:13.060
So entering this game for me was just a surprise in general,

01:21:13.060 --> 01:21:17.520
because after playing for maybe four to five hours,

01:21:17.540 --> 01:21:20.480
I thought about what would I want to change in this game,

01:21:20.480 --> 01:21:25.800
and the things that I want to change in this game are not gameplay related.

01:21:26.080 --> 01:21:28.860
They're more like just quality of life improvements,

01:21:28.860 --> 01:21:33.860
like a practice mode or a save battery or maybe...

01:21:33.860 --> 01:21:35.000
You want more of this game.

01:21:35.140 --> 01:21:35.980
Yeah, exactly.

01:21:36.540 --> 01:21:42.620
Maybe an option to choose who you fight next instead of just getting thrown into the same bracket each time.

01:21:43.400 --> 01:21:48.560
There's something about that component of it where I'm asking for more features,

01:21:48.560 --> 01:21:50.180
but not necessarily like,

01:21:50.540 --> 01:21:53.020
oh, I wish that punching mechanics would have worked this way,

01:21:53.040 --> 01:21:55.520
or I wish that you did have the freedom to move,

01:21:55.640 --> 01:22:01.480
or I wish that Little Mac was bigger in scale so I could do other things to my opponent.

01:22:01.680 --> 01:22:04.420
It doesn't matter because I'm not wishing for those things,

01:22:04.420 --> 01:22:07.660
and I think that that's the testament of an essential game.

01:22:07.660 --> 01:22:10.780
To what Sean was talking about when it comes to Super Punch-Out,

01:22:10.940 --> 01:22:12.540
the only thing I'll say there is,

01:22:12.680 --> 01:22:16.980
I don't have a problem with Punch-Out and Super Punch-Out being on an essential games list,

01:22:17.020 --> 01:22:19.620
just like I wouldn't have a problem with Super Mario Bros.

01:22:19.700 --> 01:22:21.240
1 and possibly Super Mario Bros.

01:22:21.340 --> 01:22:22.900
2, 3, World,

01:22:23.100 --> 01:22:25.080
all those other games being on the essential games list.

01:22:25.280 --> 01:22:28.340
Each game at the end of the day still speaks for itself,

01:22:28.860 --> 01:22:33.660
and I think that there is a difference between playing, say, a Pokemon Red and Blue

01:22:33.660 --> 01:22:35.640
over playing Pokemon Sun and Moon.

01:22:35.800 --> 01:22:38.360
You can say that Pokemon Sun and Moon are a million times better

01:22:38.360 --> 01:22:40.480
because they have a million more times content,

01:22:41.440 --> 01:22:45.800
but at the end of the day there's a certain atmosphere and polish to Red and Blue

01:22:45.800 --> 01:22:47.320
that Sun and Moon aren't going to offer,

01:22:47.360 --> 01:22:48.300
and that's the same thing here.

01:22:48.480 --> 01:22:52.820
There's a Wii game that takes Punch-Out and completely flips it

01:22:52.820 --> 01:22:54.460
with a Wiimote and a Nunchuck,

01:22:54.640 --> 01:22:56.160
and you're actually controlling the character,

01:22:56.160 --> 01:22:58.740
and that sounds like an awesome boxing experience,

01:22:58.840 --> 01:23:00.800
but does it translate to a great game?

01:23:01.040 --> 01:23:02.480
I don't know. I didn't play it.

01:23:02.560 --> 01:23:06.180
But I did play Mike Tyson's Punch-Out on the NES,

01:23:06.380 --> 01:23:09.060
and I'm confidently going to vote that into the essential games list,

01:23:09.080 --> 01:23:12.000
not only because I think that's where it belongs,

01:23:12.000 --> 01:23:16.660
just based on its ability to play so well in 2019,

01:23:16.660 --> 01:23:20.260
but also just because the amount of polish it has

01:23:20.260 --> 01:23:23.280
over virtually every NES game we've played so far,

01:23:23.280 --> 01:23:27.840
besides, for me, Gradius, is just insane.

01:23:28.100 --> 01:23:30.860
I think this game is way more polished than, say, Super Mario Bros.

01:23:31.160 --> 01:23:32.320
It was beautiful, Mike.

01:23:38.180 --> 01:23:40.520
So, and when I say polished for Gradius,

01:23:40.600 --> 01:23:42.020
because I know I'm going to get some flack for that,

01:23:42.180 --> 01:23:43.860
I really want people to think about

01:23:43.860 --> 01:23:48.020
all of the options and choices you have in Gradius,

01:23:48.020 --> 01:23:49.520
and I think that that's really, like,

01:23:49.520 --> 01:23:51.920
something that NES games just weren't offering.

01:23:52.140 --> 01:23:53.740
Legend of Zelda offers you an exploration

01:23:53.740 --> 01:23:55.220
that no other game can offer,

01:23:55.240 --> 01:23:57.340
but there are still some gameplay tweaks there

01:23:57.340 --> 01:23:58.680
that definitely could have been made,

01:23:58.800 --> 01:24:02.400
where, say, Gradius, just no matter how hard it is,

01:24:02.500 --> 01:24:05.260
It is Gradius. Yeah, it's still great.

01:24:05.660 --> 01:24:09.440
Yes. So I think it's games like that,

01:24:09.600 --> 01:24:11.000
and Legend of Zelda's on the essential games list,

01:24:11.000 --> 01:24:12.800
it's games like that that stand out for me.

01:24:12.900 --> 01:24:16.480
You know, we play maybe 10 to 11 bad games in a row,

01:24:16.480 --> 01:24:18.740
and then all of a sudden we get fed one that's just like,

01:24:18.860 --> 01:24:20.440
wow, I definitely still want to play this.

01:24:20.620 --> 01:24:22.580
That makes the rest of the shit games worth it.

01:24:22.720 --> 01:24:25.120
Right. One day I'd love to beat Mike Tyson.

01:24:25.260 --> 01:24:28.660
I don't know if I ever will, but I definitely see this as a game I'll keep coming back to.

01:24:28.820 --> 01:24:30.460
I do go back to Balloon Fight.

01:24:30.700 --> 01:24:32.280
You know, that's a game that I voted essential,

01:24:32.280 --> 01:24:33.340
and I still play it,

01:24:33.400 --> 01:24:35.140
and I still try to beat my high scores on that,

01:24:35.240 --> 01:24:37.180
and that's interesting, because I'm not really a high score guy.

01:24:37.700 --> 01:24:38.620
So I think...

01:24:38.620 --> 01:24:40.500
I feel like we just learned something about you.

01:24:40.580 --> 01:24:42.440
Yeah, there's a thing to me

01:24:42.880 --> 01:24:44.420
about the essential games list

01:24:44.420 --> 01:24:47.300
where I'm confident in my votes

01:24:47.300 --> 01:24:49.320
because I keep going back to those games.

01:24:49.480 --> 01:24:51.380
I felt like if I vote something in

01:24:51.380 --> 01:24:52.660
and I'm not feeling it,

01:24:52.940 --> 01:24:56.740
there is a chance that I'm going to ask for it to be removed.

01:24:57.840 --> 01:25:00.980
All right. That's a thinly veiled threat, but I get it.

01:25:01.400 --> 01:25:04.520
All right. We are done with this week's episode.

01:25:04.560 --> 01:25:05.620
End of the day. Yeah.

01:25:06.660 --> 01:25:08.000
This has been a great episode.

01:25:08.240 --> 01:25:09.940
Are you happy? I'm very happy.

01:25:10.120 --> 01:25:11.000
How are you guys feeling?

01:25:11.000 --> 01:25:13.540
Mike Tyson's Punch-Out is an essential game.

01:25:14.000 --> 01:25:15.920
Unanimously. Yes. I did want to mention,

01:25:16.040 --> 01:25:18.660
you said if Sean had voted it in,

01:25:18.720 --> 01:25:21.620
it would have been essential. Have we officially changed our rule?

01:25:21.620 --> 01:25:22.880
No, I just keep forgetting the rule.

01:25:22.880 --> 01:25:24.760
Yeah, it was already essential, but...

01:25:24.760 --> 01:25:26.960
Yeah, sorry. I keep forgetting that we had it at 50%.

01:25:26.960 --> 01:25:27.820
We definitely need to change it.

01:25:27.820 --> 01:25:29.760
Yeah, I'm in favor of changing next year.

01:25:29.760 --> 01:25:31.520
But that's something we have to talk about, yeah,

01:25:31.620 --> 01:25:33.060
in our Best of 87,

01:25:33.060 --> 01:25:35.280
which is an episode that's coming up relatively soon.

01:25:35.280 --> 01:25:37.480
We have a few more great games to get through, though.

01:25:38.340 --> 01:25:40.580
Next week, we're doing Rad Racer.

01:25:41.160 --> 01:25:44.280
Which is... Whether that's one of those games is yet to be seen.

01:25:44.540 --> 01:25:47.180
Yeah, no. Whether it's an essential game is anyone's guess.

01:25:47.320 --> 01:25:50.300
But it is a square soft game.

01:25:50.300 --> 01:25:52.100
So I think they were just going by square at that point.

01:25:52.260 --> 01:25:55.920
Still square, yeah. But, you know, that's all I have to tease about that.

01:25:56.000 --> 01:25:58.100
They made 3D Battles of World Runner,

01:25:58.260 --> 01:26:00.640
which was episode 59, if you want to go check it out.

01:26:01.560 --> 01:26:03.620
In that one, we weren't too hyped about it,

01:26:03.700 --> 01:26:06.840
but they did create a 3D on the NES.

01:26:07.100 --> 01:26:09.520
A 3D. Yeah, you know, whether it was good or not,

01:26:09.660 --> 01:26:10.600
you be the judge.

01:26:11.140 --> 01:26:14.440
And that reminds me that you can always follow along with Nostalgia

01:26:14.440 --> 01:26:16.280
by going to our Twitter account,

01:26:16.500 --> 01:26:17.980
at NostalgiaCast.

01:26:18.100 --> 01:26:21.480
We have a pinned post there that I update about once a week.

01:26:21.480 --> 01:26:22.780
I'd like to update it twice a week,

01:26:22.840 --> 01:26:23.900
but I don't see the point, really,

01:26:23.980 --> 01:26:25.980
other than posting the same thing again.

01:26:26.180 --> 01:26:27.840
But essentially what I do is I just give you

01:26:27.840 --> 01:26:29.920
the next four games that are coming up,

01:26:29.960 --> 01:26:32.600
and that gives you a whole month to play the fourth game.

01:26:32.600 --> 01:26:36.120
So definitely check out that list if you're looking forward to what's coming

01:26:36.120 --> 01:26:37.480
and want to play along with us.

01:26:37.600 --> 01:26:40.660
We do our best here to describe how these games play,

01:26:40.700 --> 01:26:42.040
but at the end of the day,

01:26:42.180 --> 01:26:44.260
you're really going to need to, at the very least,

01:26:44.360 --> 01:26:47.140
watch a video to get the gist of these NES games.

01:26:47.220 --> 01:26:52.320
They're abstract. Yeah, it's more fun to play than to listen to us describe the play.

01:26:52.440 --> 01:26:55.040
And if you're ever concerned about going back to play older games

01:26:55.040 --> 01:26:57.300
that we've already touched on the podcast,

01:26:57.920 --> 01:27:01.860
you can always check out www.nostalgiacast.com.

01:27:02.020 --> 01:27:04.540
We have an episode section where we actually have a whole column

01:27:04.540 --> 01:27:08.380
dedicated to whether you should skip it, play it, or consider it essential.

01:27:08.660 --> 01:27:09.460
They have links.

01:27:09.740 --> 01:27:12.700
There's a lot of links to other pages on the page.

01:27:12.720 --> 01:27:15.180
Yes, absolutely, where we have in-depth thoughts.

01:27:15.340 --> 01:27:16.920
We have thoughts. Lots of thoughts.

01:27:16.960 --> 01:27:20.440
Several thoughts. Most of us think every second of our lives.

01:27:20.840 --> 01:27:21.740
At least once.

01:27:21.780 --> 01:27:24.940
But us, we only think on the website, and that's where you can find it.

01:27:24.940 --> 01:27:32.980
Yep, I have a secret URL on that website that uploads my conscious stream of thoughts.

01:27:33.300 --> 01:27:35.000
And it's just like... How do you get that to work?

01:27:35.560 --> 01:27:37.420
I had to put a microchip in my brain,

01:27:37.540 --> 01:27:39.480
but thankfully the government gave it to me free of charge.

01:27:40.160 --> 01:27:48.880
Oh. Yep. In other news, you can always reach us via the contact option on the website.

01:27:48.880 --> 01:27:51.320
That's at www.nostalgiacast.com.

01:27:51.420 --> 01:27:52.260
There's a little contact form.

01:27:52.280 --> 01:27:53.920
You can fill that in, hit the send.

01:27:53.920 --> 01:27:59.240
Or you can just email us at nostalgiacast at gmail.com.

01:28:00.440 --> 01:28:02.320
That's nostalgiacast at gmail.com.

01:28:02.540 --> 01:28:04.400
You can write a letter to the website.

01:28:08.460 --> 01:28:10.720
Ask for birthday performances.

01:28:10.720 --> 01:28:11.720
We do those.

01:28:12.200 --> 01:28:18.980
Yeah. If you just put on your mail, like on your envelope, with no letter or anything in it...

01:28:18.980 --> 01:28:20.620
Just like Mike. Mike from NostalgiaCast.

01:28:20.740 --> 01:28:22.040
Yeah, Mike from NostalgiaCast.

01:28:22.040 --> 01:28:23.240
It'll find it right to him.

01:28:23.240 --> 01:28:24.600
It will probably come to me.

01:28:24.880 --> 01:28:26.540
Yeah. Is that it?

01:28:26.640 --> 01:28:27.980
All right. Yeah, that's it.

01:28:28.340 --> 01:28:29.900
Thank you guys so much for listening.

01:28:30.100 --> 01:28:33.500
Sorry if you listened to this back half, but you know the rules by now.

01:28:33.640 --> 01:28:35.780
You must listen to the entire episodes.

01:28:35.940 --> 01:28:39.820
Because otherwise, you wouldn't know this week's secret code word, which is potato.

01:28:40.580 --> 01:28:44.700
Please tweet me from only seven days from today.

01:28:45.000 --> 01:28:46.040
Tweet me potato.

01:28:46.040 --> 01:28:48.540
Potato, and I will send you...

01:28:48.540 --> 01:28:50.380
A potato. 15 nostalgia bucks.

01:28:50.700 --> 01:28:55.040
Yeah. 15 nostalgia bucks that are redeemable on the store's website.

01:28:55.240 --> 01:28:56.920
Yes. The store's website.

01:28:59.980 --> 01:29:02.560
Where, who knows, maybe you can purchase a domain.

